Quick summary (high level)
- You should report: W-2 wages ($84,900), self‑employment income from web design ($11,200) on Schedule C, and the interest/other brokerage income (the $920 and $550) on Form 1040 (and Schedule B only if interest/dividend total > $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts).
- Student loan interest: you can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line). You reported $2,700 paid — the maximum deductible amount is $2,500 if you otherwise qualify.
- Charitable gift: $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There was no above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023, so if you take the standard deduction you do not get this deduction separately on the federal return.
- Unreimbursed expenses: if these are expenses related to the self‑employment/web design work, they belong on Schedule C as business expenses (deductible). If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- Home office: since you used a dedicated 140 sq ft space exclusively for your web‑design business, you can take a self‑employment home office deduction. You can choose either the simplified method ($5 × 140 = $700) or the actual expenses method (pro rata share of rent, utilities, etc.). Using actual allocation for rent alone: 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47% of annual rent ($2,400 × 12 = $28,800) → about $4,740 of rent allocated to the home office (plus prorated utilities/insurance if included). If you use actual expenses you generally file Form 8829 with Schedule C. The home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and therefore reduces income subject to income and self‑employment tax).
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe self‑employment tax (Schedule SE) on net self‑employment earnings; half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Itemize vs standard: for 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700. Based on the items you listed (only $1,350 charitable and no mortgage interest or large state/property taxes mentioned), you will almost certainly get more tax benefit from the standard deduction. The home office and business expenses reduce your self‑employment income regardless of whether you itemize.
- State: you’ll file Oregon state return (Form OR‑40 for residents). Oregon generally starts from federal AGI but has some differences — you’ll still file a state return and carry federal adjustments forward as required.

Detailed notes and calculations (illustrative)
1) Income
- W-2 wages: $84,900 (Form W‑2 → Form 1040)
- Self‑employment (web design): $11,200 → Schedule C (gross receipts). Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C (home office, supplies, software, internet portion, client expenses if any).
- Interest/brokerage: $920 + $550 = $1,470 — treat as interest or dividends depending on the 1099s you have. If they’re interest (1099‑INT) and combined total is under $1,500, you do not need Schedule B. If any is a 1099‑B (stock sales) you’ll report capital gains on Schedule D/Form 8949 as required.

2) Home office (two methods)
- Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700. No Form 8829. Simpler recordkeeping.
- Actual (example using only rent): Rent $2,400 × 12 = $28,800 × (140/850 ≈ 0.1647) = ≈ $4,743. Include prorated utilities/renter’s insurance if you want. If you use actual expenses you should complete Form 8829 and attach with Schedule C. Note: The home office deduction cannot be taken for an employee’s W‑2 job (post‑2017 rules) — only for self‑employed/independent contractor activity.

3) Student loan interest
- Maximum deductible amount for 2023 is $2,500 (adjustment to income). You reported paying $2,700; you can deduct up to $2,500 provided your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) is below the phaseout threshold. For Married Filing Jointly the phaseout range for 2023 begins at $155,000 and fully phases out at $185,000. Based on your reported income (~$97k before deductions), you should be fully eligible for the $2,500 deduction.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these were costs you incurred as part of the web design work (supplies, software, client expenses), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your business net income.
- If they were unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025.

5) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- After Schedule C net profit is calculated, use Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax (15.3% on net earnings, with the 92.35% factor applied). Half of SE tax is deductible on Schedule 1 (adjustment to income).
- Also consider the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). With your small self‑employment income, you’ll likely qualify for some QBI deduction, but the exact amount depends on taxable income after deductions etc.

6) Itemize vs standard
- Standard deduction MFJ for 2023: $27,700.
