--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 7824 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — good detail. Below I’ll (A) summarize what’s likely deductible, (B) show a worked example with both home-office methods so you can compare, (C) recommend whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, (D) list the forms you’ll need, and (E) next steps / questions so I can refine numbers.

A — What you can (generally) deduct
- Self-employment/business expenses (web design): deductible on Schedule C. This includes supplies, software, hosting, the “unreimbursed expenses” if they are business expenses (not employee unreimbursed expenses). Employee unreimbursed expenses are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Home-office: deductible only if you use the space regularly and exclusively for the business and it is your principal place of business. You may use:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft (140 sq ft × $5 = $700), or
  - Actual-expense method: prorate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) by business-use percentage (140/850).
  You must choose the method that gives the larger deduction; if you use the actual method you file Form 8829; simplified method does not use Form 8829.
- Student loan interest: an above-the-line deduction (Form 1040 adjustment) — up to $2,500 for 2023, subject to MAGI phaseouts. You’ll need Form 1098-E from your servicer (FedLoan).
- Charitable cash donation ($1,350): deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Keep your receipt.
- Interest / investment income (Goldman Sachs Marcus $920; TD Ameritrade $550): taxable interest/dividends. Report on Form 1040; if total interest/dividends > $1,500 for the year you’ll also complete Schedule B.
- Self-employment tax: you pay SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self-employment earnings; half of that SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income.

B — Quick worked example (estimates) — you can supply more detail if you want exact numbers

Inputs you gave:
- W-2 wages: $84,900
- Self-employment gross (web design): $11,200
- Interest (Goldman): $920
- TD Ameritrade: $550
- Home: 140 sq ft exclusive out of 850 sq ft total
- Rent: $2,400/mo → annual rent = $28,800
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (max deductible is $2,500)
- Charitable cash: $1,350
- Unreimbursed expenses: $1,350 — treated here as business expenses (if they are business expenses)

Option 1 — Simplified home-office method
- Home-office deduction = 140 sq ft × $5 = $700
- Business expenses (other) = $1,350
- Total Schedule C expenses = $700 + $1,350 = $2,050
- Schedule C net profit = $11,200 − $2,050 = $9,150
- Self-employment tax:
  - Net earnings for SE tax = 9,150 × 0.9235 ≈ 8,453
  - SE tax ≈ 8,453 × 0.153 ≈ $1,294
  - Deductible half of SE tax ≈ $647 (above-the-line)
- Adjusted Gross Income (rough):
  - Total income = 84,900 + 9,150 + (920 + 550) = 95,520
  - Subtract half SE tax (≈647) and student loan interest (up to $2,500, subject to MAGI limits)
  - AGI ≈ 95,520 − 647 − 2,500 = 92,373
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2023) = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ 64,673

Option 2 — Actual-expense method (rent only shown; adds utility share if you have them)
- Business portion of rent = 28,800 × (140 / 850) ≈ $4,744
- If you have any utilities/insurance you would add the business share of those too.
- Business expenses = $4,744 + $1,350 = $6,094
- Schedule C net profit = 11,200 − 6,094 = $5,106
- SE tax:
  - Net earnings for SE tax = 5,106 × 0.9235 ≈ 4,713
  - SE tax ≈ 4,713 × 0.153 ≈ $721
  - Deductible half SE tax ≈ $361
- AGI rough:
  - Total income = 84,900 + 5,106 + 1,470 = 91,476
  - Minus half SE tax (≈361) and student loan interest (up to $2,500) = AGI ≈ 88,615
- Standard deduction = $27,700 → taxable income ≈ 60,915

Which home-office method looks better?
- In the example above, the actual-expense method (rent allocation only) gives a bigger home-office deduction (~$4,744) vs simplified $700, reducing taxable income more. However, actual-method requires Form 8829 and more recordkeeping. If you have other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance) the actual method becomes even more favorable.
- Note: the home-office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit; it cannot be taken if the home office is not regular and exclusive.

C — Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your only itemized amount you listed is charitable contributions = $1,350. Other common itemized items (mortgage interest, property tax, state income taxes) were not listed.
- With only $1,350 in charitable donations and no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes, your itemized total will be well below the standard deduction. Recommendation: take the standard deduction unless you have additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, significant state & local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, etc.).

D — Forms you’ll need (federal) and when to use them
- Form 1040 — main return (Married Filing Jointly).
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — reports additional income/adjustments if applicable.
- Schedule C (Form 1040) — Profit or Loss from Business (report web design income and business expenses).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (computes SE tax).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home IF you use the actual-expense method for the home office. Not needed for the simplified method.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you itemize).
- Schedule B — Interest and Ordinary Dividends (if total taxable interest + dividends > $1,500).
- Form 1098-E — student loan interest statement from FedLoan (you’ll need it to substantiate the student loan interest deduction).
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — if your clients issued them (reportable on Schedule C).
- 1099-INT and 1099-DIV / 1099-B from TD Ameritrade — report interest/dividends/capital gains as applicable.
