Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction on your federal return (2023 MFJ standard deduction = $27,700). Your itemizable deductions (charity $1,350 and any other itemizable items you didn’t list) are far smaller than the standard deduction.
- Report your web‑design income on Schedule C. That’s where business income, the unreimbursed expenses (if they are business expenses), and the home‑office deduction belong.
- For the home office, the regular method (use Form 8829) will give a much larger deduction than the simplified method for the numbers you gave. The home office deduction is limited to net profit from the business.
- You’ll also need Schedule SE (self‑employment tax). Student loan interest (up to $2,500) is an above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1 if you qualify by MAGI.
- Federal forms you’ll likely use: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Form 8829 (if you use the regular home‑office method). If you itemize (unlikely) add Schedule A. You’ll also file your Oregon state return (OR form — OR-40 for residents) and follow OR rules.

Detailed explanation and numbers (assumptions noted)
1) Income to report
- W-2 (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900 (reported on Form 1040)
- Web design: $11,200 — report on Schedule C (business income). Report even if you did not receive 1099s.
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $550 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,470 — taxable interest reported on Form 1040 (Schedule B only if total interest > $1,500).
Total gross income (before adjustments) ≈ $97,570.

2) Student loan interest
- You paid $2,700 in student loan interest. The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line), subject to MAGI phaseout.
- Phaseout for married filing jointly (2023): starts at MAGI $145,000 and is gone at $175,000. Based on the numbers above your MAGI is under the phaseout threshold, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction (you’d deduct $2,500 on Schedule 1 unless your MAGI is higher for other reasons).
- Expect a Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid.

3) Charitable donation
- $1,350 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank. You have the receipt, which you need if the amount is over $250 (contemporaneous written acknowledgement).
- Charitable deductions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Since the standard deduction for MFJ is $27,700, it is unlikely to be beneficial to itemize just to claim $1,350 in donations.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job: under the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (2018–2025) miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible on your federal return. So they would not reduce federal taxable income.
- If the $1,350 are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your web‑design work (supplies, software, hosting, client travel, etc.), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your self‑employment net income. Please confirm whether these are business expenses for the web‑design work.

5) Home‑office deduction (you said: 140 sq ft exclusive home office; home = 850 sq ft; rent $2,400/month)
- Office % = 140 / 850 = 16.47% (≈16.5%).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Regular method: deductible portion of rent = 28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,744. (Plus a proportional share of utilities, renters insurance, etc., if you pay them; depreciation isn’t relevant to renters.)
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.
- So regular method gives a much larger deduction (~$4,744 vs $700). Use Form 8829 to calculate the regular‑method deduction and attach it to Schedule C. Note: the home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from that business — you can’t use it to create or increase a loss beyond limits (excess may carry over in some cases).
- Example with numbers (if the $1,350 are business expenses):
  - Gross web income 11,200
  - Business expenses (other) 1,350
  - Net before home office = 9,850
  - Home office regular deduction ≈ 4,744 → net profit ≈ 5,106
  - Home office simplified deduction = 700 → net profit ≈ 9,150
  (These are illustrative — include utilities/other business expenses if applicable.)

6) Self‑employment tax and related deduction
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. SE tax = 15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings; you compute it on Schedule SE.
- Half of your SE tax is an “above‑the‑line” deduction on Schedule 1 (it reduces AGI).
- You also pay income tax on the net business profit (after the Schedule 1 adjustments and standard deduction).

7) Whether to itemize or take the standard deduction
- Federal (2023): Standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700. Your itemizable federal deductions you listed are $1,350 (charity) + maybe state and local taxes and mortgage interest/property taxes (not provided). Unless you have substantial other deductible amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, significant medical expenses above threshold, large charitable donations), you will take the standard deduction.
- If you do not itemize federally, that does not necessarily force the same choice on Oregon — state rules differ. You will file an Oregon resident return (OR‑40) and follow Oregon’s rules for deductions. If you itemize federally, Oregon typically requires you to start with federal itemized. If you take the federal standard deduction, Oregon has its own standard/other adjustments — check OR instructions or ask if you want me to look up the OR specifics.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for student loan interest adjustment, and certain other items
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for web‑design income/expenses
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the regular home‑office method; if you use simplified, do not file Form 8829
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax and the deductible half
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (unlikely)
- Schedule B — only if your interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500 or you have certain foreign accounts
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — informational from lender
- 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC: you may receive these from clients; even if you don’t receive them, you must report the income

9) Practical next steps I recommend
- Confirm whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for your web design work. If yes, treat them on Schedule C; if no (employee expenses), they are not deductible federally.
- Use the regular home office method (Form 8829) — it will almost certainly give a bigger deduction given your rent amount and the office percent.
- Keep receipts and contemporaneous records: donation acknowledgement (required if > $250), 1098‑E for student loan interest, receipts for business expenses, a floorplan or documentation showing exclusive use of the office, and copies of rent/utility bills showing amounts paid.
- Check your employer withholding to ensure you don’t underpay estimated taxes (you can adjust W‑4 or make estimated payments if you expect you’ll owe > $1,000 after withholding).
- File your Oregon resident return (OR‑40) — I can give specifics for OR if you want.

Caveats and recommendation
- This is general guidance based on the facts you gave and 2023 federal rules. State rules (Oregon) differ; if you want I can look up the Oregon forms and whether itemizing differently at the state level might help.
- For precise calculations, preparing your return (or having software/CPA compute exact AGI, taxable income, SE tax, and phaseouts) is recommended. If you’d like, I can run the numbers using either the regular or simplified home‑office method and show estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax (I’ll just need confirmation on whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses and whether utilities or other home costs should be included).

Would you like me to:
- (A) estimate your federal tax and SE tax both ways (regular vs simplified home office) using the assumptions above, or
- (B) proceed to prepare a checklist of documents and exact lines/forms to fill, or
- (C) look up Oregon (OR‑40) specifics for state filing?

