Thanks — this is enough to give a clear, practical answer and a short checklist of next steps. I’ll (1) summarize what you can deduct, (2) compare itemizing vs taking the standard deduction, (3) explain how to treat the home office, (4) list the federal (and Oregon) forms you’ll likely need, and (5) give next steps / questions so I can refine the numbers.

1) What you can (likely) deduct
- Student loan interest: You can claim the student loan interest deduction (an adjustment to income) up to $2,500 for 2023. You reported $2,700 paid, so your allowable deduction will be capped at $2,500 unless your MAGI for MFJ exceeds the phaseout range (starts at $145,000 and phases out completely at $175,000 for 2023). Based on the incomes you posted, you’re under the phaseout range, so you should get the full $2,500.
- Charitable contributions: The $1,350 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize. Keep the receipt (cash/check/credit card or written acknowledgment).
- Self-employment business expenses: Your web design income ($11,200) is self-employment income and you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses against that on Schedule C (e.g., software, hosting, supplies, the deductible portion of your home office, and any business mileage). If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” you listed are business expenses for the web design work, they are deductible on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 because miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended by the TCJA.
- Interest from Goldman/TDA: The interest ($920 + $550 = $1,470) is taxable ordinary income and reported on your 1040 (you’ll get 1099-INTs).
- Home office: See next section — deductible portion belongs on Schedule C (two methods).
- Self-employment tax: You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net SE income (Schedule SE). You can deduct one-half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 / Form 1040 adjustments).

2) Itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your likely itemizable federal deductions (as listed now) are: $1,350 charitable contribution plus possibly state/local taxes (SALT) up to $10,000 if you paid state income tax or property tax (you didn’t provide SALT or mortgage interest amounts). With only $1,350 of obvious itemized deductions, itemizing will produce a much smaller deduction than the $27,700 standard deduction.
- Conclusion: For federal taxes you will almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have additional (not yet listed) itemizable deductions (large medical expenses, mortgage interest, substantial state/local taxes up to $10k combined, large charitable gifts, etc.). Taking the standard deduction is the likely best choice.

3) Home office — how to treat it
- You qualify for a home office deduction only for the self-employment/web design business if the space is used regularly and exclusively for that business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft → 140 × $5 = $700. No Form 8829 required. Simpler recordkeeping.
  - Regular method: Allocate a percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.) to the home office. Your office is 140/850 = 16.47% of the home. At $2,400/month rent = $28,800/year; allocated rent = about $28,800 × 0.1647 ≈ $4,744. You would report the deductible portion on Schedule C using Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) to calculate allowable deduction. Because you rent, you cannot take depreciation for the residence, but you can deduct the allocated part of rent and utilities.
- Which to choose: Based only on rent and the 140 sq ft figure, the regular method gives a larger deduction (~$4,744) vs. the simplified ($700). Use the regular method if you want the bigger deduction and you can substantiate rent/utility amounts. If you have no other home-related records or want simplicity, the simplified method is fine.
- Note: Home office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit. If it creates or increases a loss, the loss rules apply (loss could offset other income but may be limited by self-employment/business loss rules).

4) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return).
- Schedule 1 (if your software shows it separately) — for adjustments to income (student loan interest, half of SE tax).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report web-design income and business expenses.
- Schedule SE — to compute self-employment tax on net SE income.
- Form 8829 — if you use the regular method for the home office (calculate allowable home office deduction and transfer result to Schedule C).
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — if you qualify and want to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (20% of qualified business income). Most taxpayers with taxable income below the higher thresholds use Form 8995 (the simpler form); your small business income will likely qualify for at least some QBI deduction.
- Forms/Docs to collect: W-2 from Desert Sun Logistics, 1099-INTs (Marcus & TD Ameritrade), any 1099-NEC/1099-MISC from web-design clients (even if you don’t receive a 1099 you still report the income), Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) or other documentation from your loan servicer, receipts for charitable contributions, and receipts/invoices for business expenses.
- Oregon state: Oregon resident return (Form OR-40). Oregon starts from federal AGI and then you make state modifications; Oregon has its own rules re: deductions and credits. You’ll choose the Oregon standard deduction or itemize on the state return per Oregon rules. I can walk you through specifics if you want.

5) Quick example (rough numbers) — to show impact
Assumptions: W-2 $84,900 + SE $11,200 + interest $1,470 = $97,570 total income.
- If home office uses regular method on rent: home-office = ~$4,744, net SE income ≈ $11,200 − $4,744 = $6,456.
  - SE tax ≈ 15.3% × 0.9235 × $6,456 ≈ $913; half SE tax deduction ≈ $456.
  - Student loan interest deduction = $2,500 (max allowed).
  - Adjustments to income ≈ $2,500 + $456 = $2,956.
  - AGI ≈ $97,570 − $2,956 = $94,614.
  - Standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $66,914 (this is a rough illustration).
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are actually business expenses, they lower net SE income further and reduce SE tax / increase deductions.
- These are approximate numbers to illustrate flow; exact tax and whether taking standard vs itemize depends on any missing items (mortgage interest, property taxes, additional charitable gifts, etc.).

6) What I recommend / next steps
- Decide whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are employee (W-2) expenses or business expenses for the web design work. If they are business expenses, gather receipts and plan to deduct them on Schedule C.
- Choose home office method:
  - If you want the larger deduction and have rent/utility records, use the regular method and prepare Form 8829 (you’ll likely get a much bigger deduction than the simplified method).
  - If you prefer simplicity, choose the simplified method ($700).
- Gather documents: W-2, all 1099s, Form 1098-E (student loan interest), receipts for charitable gifts, rent/utility bills (if using regular home-office method), receipts for business expenses, and any state tax/payment records.
- File federal Form 1040 with Schedules C and SE; file OR-40 for Oregon state return.
- Consider whether you need to adjust withholding or make estimated tax payments for 2024 if you expect similar SE income (self-employment tax / income tax may not be covered by W-2 withholding).

If you’d like, I can:
- Run a more exact calculation for federal tax owed using either home office method if you confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business-related and whether you want to use the regular or simplified home-office calculation.
- Prepare a checklist of the exact documents you need to upload to a tax preparer or tax software.
- Draft an email to your accountant with the numbers and questions to clarify before filing.

Which of these would you like me to do next? If you want exact tax estimates, tell me whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses and whether you want to use the simplified or regular home-office method.

