1) What you can deduct (federal, general rules)
- Self‑employment income and related business expenses
  - Report the web‑design income and business expenses on Schedule C (Form 1040).
  - Business expenses reduce your Schedule C net profit and are subject to self‑employment tax.
  - If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses (supplies, software, client costs, etc. for the web design work), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on your federal return for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
- Home office
  - Two methods: simplified or regular (actual).
    - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (report on Schedule C).
    - Actual (preferred if you have sizable rent/utilities to allocate): business percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%. Applied to annual rent $28,800 → ≈ $4,744 of rent attributable to the home office. You could also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., using the same percentage. To use actual you’d generally file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C.
  - Important limits: home‑office deductions cannot create/increase a deductible business loss for the year beyond certain limits; if your home office expenses exceed net profit, some of the disallowed portion may be carried forward to future years (see Pub. 587). If the actual home‑office share (≈ $4.7k) exceeds your gross self‑employment income after other business expenses, the excess may be limited.
- Student loan interest
  - You may deduct up to $2,500 of qualified student loan interest as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1). Because you paid $2,700, the maximum deductible is $2,500 (the $200 excess is not deductible). There is an income phaseout for MFJ (AGI between $145k–$175k for 2023), and you appear below that based on the amounts given, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500.
- Charitable contribution
  - $1,350 cash gift is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not get a separate deduction for this gift on the federal return.
- Interest income
  - Report $1,470 interest on Form 1040 (no Schedule B required unless total interest > $1,500). You’ll receive 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV for these amounts from the institutions.

2) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for MFJ = $27,700.
- Your only stated itemizable federal deduction is $1,350 charitable (plus any state/local taxes and mortgage interest you haven’t listed). Unless you have significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses (>7.5% of AGI), or other itemizable amounts, the standard deduction will be far larger than your likely Schedule A total. So with the information given, you should take the standard deduction.
- Note: home office deduction is NOT part of Schedule A — it’s a business expense on Schedule C and does not affect the standard vs itemize decision directly.

3) Home office — recommendation and practical effect
- If you only want a small, simple deduction and minimal paperwork, the simplified method gives $700.
- If you can document rent and utilities and want the larger deduction, actual method will give about $4,744 of rent allocated to the office (plus any utilities/insurance share). That will lower your Schedule C net income by that amount, which:
  - Lowers income subject to income tax,
  - Lowers net income subject to self‑employment tax,
  - But cannot increase an allowable business loss beyond IRS limits; excess home‑office allocation may be disallowed/carryforward if it pushes your business into a loss.
- Which to choose: actual will likely give a larger reduction unless the allocation would be limited by low business net income. If your business has limited other expenses and total gross of $11,200, actual rent allocation (~$4.7k) plus other expenses may be okay, but if it would create a loss, some of the deduction could be deferred.

4) Self‑employment tax and other mechanics
- Net self‑employment income is subject to self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare, roughly 15.3%), computed on Schedule SE. You may deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 (above the line).
- You should check whether you need to make quarterly estimated tax payments for 2024 if withholding doesn’t cover income and self‑employment tax.

5) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction (and half of SE tax deduction appears here)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — web design income and expenses, including the home office deduction (if using simplified it’s claimed on Schedule C; if using actual you generally complete Form 8829 and transfer allowed amount)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if using the actual (not simplified) method for the home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize (doesn’t look beneficial given current items)
- Schedule B — only if total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500 (your interest is $1,470, so probably not needed)
- Keep/collect: W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics; 1099‑NEC or records of the $11,200 self‑employment receipts; 1099‑INT(s) from Marcus/TD Ameritrade; receipts for the charitable gift and for student-loan interest (Form 1098‑E if provided).

6) Oregon (state) filing
- You’ll also file Oregon state return (OR‑40 or the correct Oregon form). Oregon follows most federal definitions but has differences; charitable and other state tax treatments can differ. If you itemize federally you might have different state adjustments — check Oregon instructions or consult a preparer.

7) Things I recommend you do next
- Confirm whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are:
  - Business expenses for the web‑design business (put on Schedule C), or
  - Unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job (not deductible federally for 2018–2025).
- Decide whether to use the simplified home‑office method ($700) or actual (likely ~ $4,744 of rent plus utilities). If you want, I can run the numbers both ways including how home‑office affects net SE income, SE tax, and federal tax.
- Gather these documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC or records of the $11,200, all 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV, student loan interest statement (1098‑E or lender statement), charitable receipt, and records of rent/utilities if you plan to use actual home‑office method.
- Consider estimating your federal tax and self‑employment tax or increasing withholding/quarterly payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

8) Limitations and final note
- This is general guidance. Tax results can change based on additional facts (other income, spouse’s income/deductions, exact nature of the unreimbursed expenses, any 1099s you haven’t mentioned). For complex situations or to optimize for state conformity and carryovers, consider a tax preparer or CPA.

If you want, I can:
- Run estimated tax calculations both ways (simplified vs actual home office) using the figures you gave, showing estimated federal income tax and self‑employment tax.
- Or help draft a checklist of documents to gather for filing or for your preparer.

