What you can (generally) deduct
- Self‑employment (web design) business expenses: If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses (supplies, software, travel, etc. for the web design work) you can deduct them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses (you were an employee for some other employer), those miscellaneous itemized deductions were suspended for 2018–2025 and are not deductible on your federal return.
- Home office: Deductible only if the space is used regularly and exclusively for your self‑employment business (web design). Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829).
  - Actual method: allocate a percentage of actual home expenses to the business. Your business percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%. With monthly rent $2,400 (annual $28,800) the rent portion allocable to the office = $28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,744. If you also pay utilities/insurance, those would also be pro‑rated. For renters you do not depreciate the structure; you include your share of rent/utilities. Actual method generally gives a larger deduction here because your rent is high.
- Self‑employment tax: You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C income (Social Security + Medicare). You may deduct half of that SE tax as an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040 (this reduces AGI).
- Student loan interest: The federal deduction max is $2,500 per year (above‑the‑line on Schedule 1) and phases out at higher MAGI. Based on the income amounts you provided, you should be well below the phaseout threshold and able to claim the full allowed amount ($2,500). You need Form 1098‑E (or your loan statements) showing interest paid in the tax year.
- Charitable contributions: Cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You have a $1,350 cash contribution and you have the receipt; that supports the deduction if you itemize. For cash donations $250+ you must have a contemporaneous written acknowledgement from the charity (you said you have a receipt).
- Bank/broker income: Interest and dividends are taxable on Form 1040. Brokerage proceeds (sales) may generate 1099‑B and capital gains/losses reported on Schedule D/Form 8949 as needed.

Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly = $27,700.
- Your identifiable itemized deductions (as given) are small: charitable $1,350. You didn’t list mortgage interest, property tax, or large state/local taxes that would push itemized totals above $27,700.
- Conclusion: With the information provided, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for federal taxes rather than itemizing on Schedule A. The home office deduction is separate (it reduces your Schedule C net profit) and does not depend on whether you itemize or take the standard deduction.

