- Likely result: take the standard deduction, report business items on Schedule C/Schedule SE, claim home office (actual method will likely give a larger deduction than the simplified method), claim student loan interest (up to $2,500). You will probably also be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction on the Schedule C profit.
- Key federal forms: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), Form 8995 (QBI) or 8995‑A if needed, Schedule A only if you itemize (unlikely). Oregon: OR‑40 (resident return) and any Oregon schedules as required.

Details and reasoning

1) Where each item gets reported
- W‑2 wages ($84,900): Form 1040 (you’ll attach your W‑2).
- Self‑employment income ($11,200 from web design): Report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). On Schedule C you list gross receipts and business expenses (unreimbursed business expenses and home office allocation).
- Unreimbursed business expenses ($1,350): If these are ordinary and necessary business expenses for the web‑design work, they are Schedule C expenses (not employee “unreimbursed expenses”; those are not deductible for most employees).
- Home‑office: Deduct on Schedule C. You can use:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700.
  - Actual method: allocate allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs related to the business space, etc.) by business-use percentage (140 ÷ 850 ≈ 16.47%). For rent of $2,400/month = $28,800/year, the rent portion allocated to the office ≈ $4,742. You can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. (If actual deductions allocated to the office exceed business net profit, some of the excess may be limited and carried forward — see Form 8829 instructions.)
  - For self‑employed taxpayers, the actual method is reported on Form 8829 (filed with your return). The simplified method is entered directly on Schedule C.
- Interest income ($920 and $550): Report on Form 1040 (Schedule B only required if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500; your $1,470 is under that threshold).
- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid): Deduct up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1 as an adjustment to arrive at AGI). There is a MAGI phaseout (see next item).
- Charitable contribution ($1,350): If you itemize, it goes on Schedule A. With MFJ 2023 standard deduction $27,700, that $1,350 will not make you itemize — so most likely take the standard.

2) Student loan interest deduction and phaseout
- Maximum deduction is $2,500 for 2023. The phaseout for married filing jointly starts at MAGI ≈ $145,000 and is eliminated at $175,000. With the income you reported (W‑2 + self‑employment + interest), you are well under the phaseout start, so you should be able to deduct up to $2,500. (You reported $2,700 paid; only $2,500 max deductible.)

3) Home office — which method to choose
- Simplified: $700 (140 sq ft × $5). Easy, fewer records.
- Actual: ~16.47% of allowable housing expenses. Rent alone yields ≈ $4,742. Adding utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. will increase that. Actual method is likely larger in your situation.
- Caution: home office deduction must be for exclusive and regular use and for business. It cannot create or increase a business loss in some cases; excess actual home office deductions may be limited and carried forward (Form 8829 handles the computation).
- Recommendation: actual method probably gives a bigger deduction for you — but if you want simplicity and no Form 8829, choose simplified. If you want me to compute an estimate, I can run numbers both ways.

4) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (reported on Schedule SE). You can deduct half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you are likely eligible for the 20% deduction of your qualified business income (Form 8995) given your income level. QBI is calculated on net Schedule C profit (after the home office and ordinary business expenses), subject to limitations. The QBI deduction can materially reduce taxable income.

5) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your only itemizable deduction that you listed is $1,350 charitable. Unless you have sizable state and local taxes, mortgage interest, or large medical expenses not mentioned, itemizing is unlikely to exceed $27,700. So take the standard deduction.

6) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (business income from Schedule C flows here; student loan interest (adjustment) and half of SE tax are also reported here).
- Schedule C — report web‑design income and business expenses (including unreimbursed expenses).
- Schedule SE — calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use actual home‑office method (allocates home expenses to business).
- Form 8995 (or Form 8995‑A if your QBI calculation is complex) — for the 20% Qualified Business Income deduction.
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (unlikely).
- Keep: W‑2, 1099‑INTs (interest), 1098‑E (student loan interest statement, if issued), receipts for charitable donation, records of business expenses and home office calculation.
- Oregon: OR‑40 (resident return) — Oregon uses your federal AGI as the starting point; attach any Oregon schedules required. Check Oregon rules for itemized deductions, credits, or adjustments. If you owe significant tax at federal level, check Oregon estimated payments rules too.

7) Other practical points & recordkeeping
- If you didn’t receive 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, you still must report the income. Keep invoices, bank deposit records, and other documentation.
- Keep the charity receipt (you said you have it) in case of future questions — cash donations are substantiated by receipts.
- Keep your student loan interest statement (1098‑E) or ledger showing interest paid.
- If your withholding on the W‑2 doesn’t cover your combined income tax + self‑employment tax, you may owe estimated taxes and/or a penalty. If you expect to owe more than $1,000 when you file, consider making estimated payments (Form 1040‑ES) the following year.
- Make sure home‑office space is exclusive and regular use — document square footage (photos/floorplan) and how you computed shared expenses.

8) Short example estimate (illustrative)
