Short answer / bottom line
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) instead of itemizing — your only itemizable deduction you mentioned is a $1,350 charitable gift, which is far less than the standard deduction.
- Your web‑design work is self‑employment income and must be reported on Schedule C. You can (and should) take a home‑office deduction if the space meets the IRS rules. Using the actual (allocation) method for the home office will produce the biggest tax benefit given your rent.
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest (not the full $2,700 you paid) as an adjustment to income if you (and your spouse) meet the income limits (your AGI will be well below the MFJ phase‑out for 2023).
- You will owe self‑employment tax on your net self‑employment income (Schedule SE); half of that SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction.

Required federal forms (what you’ll need)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $11,200 web‑design income and business expenses (including home‑office expense)
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax
- Schedule 1 — for adjustments to income (student‑loan interest and the deductible half of self‑employment tax; note net profit from Schedule C flows to Schedule 1)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (doesn’t look beneficial here)
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (if you received them from clients) and Form W‑2 (Desert Sun Logistics) and Form(s) 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV from Goldman Sachs / TD Ameritrade (you’ll report interest/dividends on Form 1040)
- Oregon state return (OR‑40) — Oregon tax return uses your federal AGI as the starting point; you must file state tax forms for Oregon

What you can deduct and how to treat each item
1) W‑2 wages: report exactly as on your W‑2 (no special deductions here).
2) Self‑employment income (web design — $11,200):
   - Report gross receipts on Schedule C.
   - Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. From your notes:
     - $1,350 unreimbursed expenses — treat these as business expenses on Schedule C (they are deductible because you’re self‑employed; unreimbursed employee expenses would not be deductible, but that’s not the case here).
     - Home‑office deduction — treated as a business expense on Schedule C (see below).
   - Because net earnings from self‑employment > $400, you must file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net earnings after the 7.65% adjustment); you also get to deduct 50% of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
3) Home office: you have a 140 sq ft exclusive office in an 850 sq ft home (16.47% of home).
   - Two methods:
     a) Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (easy, no allocation of rent/utilities).
     b) Regular/actual method: allocate a share of rent and allowable home expenses to the business. Example using rent only: annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Office share = 140 / 850 = 16.4706% → rent portion = $28,800 × 16.4706% ≈ $4,744. You may also include proportional utility amounts, renters insurance, repairs, etc., if you have them and can substantiate them.
   - Important limits: under the regular method the home‑office deduction cannot exceed gross income from the business minus other business deductions (other than the home office). Any disallowed amount can generally be carried forward.
