Short answer up front
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2023 (married filing jointly = $27,700) unless you have additional itemizable deductions not listed here. With the items you listed, standard deduction gives the lowest federal tax.
- Your web‑design income is self‑employment income: you must report it on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), and you can claim a home‑office deduction for the 140 sq ft exclusive office. You can choose either the simplified home‑office method ($5/sq ft = $700) or the actual method (a pro‑rata share of rent/utilities). The actual method looks more favorable given your rent, but it requires better documentation (see details below).
- Student loan interest: you reported $2,700 paid, but the federal deduction limit is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher incomes). Your income is well below the phaseout, so you can take the full $2,500 as an adjustment to income.
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: if those are business expenses for the web design work, they’re deductible on Schedule C. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses (for work as an employee), they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.
- Forms you’ll use (federal): Form 1040, Schedule 1 (adjustments to income), Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Form 8829 (if you use the actual home‑office method), Schedule A (only if you itemize), and possibly Schedule B (only if your interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500). Oregon: resident return (Form OR‑40).

Detailed walkthrough and rough numbers
1) Gather these documents
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics (you already listed wages $84,900).
- 1099‑NEC or records for the $11,200 web design income (report even if no 1099).
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV from Goldman Sachs and TD Ameritrade (you listed $920 and $550).
- Form 1098‑E from FedLoan (student loan interest) — shows interest paid (you said $2,700).
- Charitable receipt for St. Mary’s Food Bank ($1,350).
- Proof of rent and utilities (rent receipts, canceled checks, bank statements) and documentation showing exclusive use of the 140 sq ft office (floor plan, photos).
- Records for the $1,350 of “unreimbursed expenses” (detail what they are and whether they relate to the web design business).

2) How to treat each item
- W‑2 wages: reported on Form 1040 (with withholdings applied).
- Self‑employment (web design): report gross receipts on Schedule C, subtract allowable business expenses (office, supplies, fees, the portion of rent/utilities used for the home office if you use the actual method, etc.) to get net profit (this net flows to Form 1040).
- Self‑employment tax: computed on Schedule SE (15.3% of roughly 92.35% of net self‑employment income). You then deduct half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- Home office:
  - Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829). Simple and quick.
  - Actual method: business percentage = 140 / 850 = 16.4706%. Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Business share of rent ≈ $28,800 × 16.4706% ≈ $4,743. You may also allocate business share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Actual method requires Form 8829 and good documentation.
  - You must meet “exclusive and regular use” tests (you said the office is exclusive).
- Student loan interest: maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (your $2,700 payment → $2,500 deductible) if your MAGI is under the phaseout limit. Based on the income shown here, you’ll be under the phaseout and can take the full $2,500.
- Charitable gift $1,350: deductible only if you itemize (reported on Schedule A). Because the MFJ standard deduction is $27,700, a lone $1,350 charitable gift will not make itemizing beneficial unless you have many other itemizable items (state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, etc.).
- “Unreimbursed expenses”:
  - If they are business expenses related to the web design work → deduct on Schedule C (we used that assumption in the calculations below).
  - If they are employee unreimbursed expenses (W‑2 job) → not deductible for 2018–2025.

3) Rough federal tax comparison (estimates) — shows impact of simplified vs actual home office
Assumptions used: interest totals reported as taxable interest (920 + 550 = 1,470), business expenses = $1,350 (you listed), no other itemizable deductions, no tax withholdings shown (I’m showing tax before withholding credits). These are estimates to show relative effects — they are not a full return.

A. Simplified home office method
- Self‑employment gross receipts: $11,200
- Business expenses: $1,350 + simplified home office $700 = $2,050
- Net business profit: 11,200 − 2,050 = $9,150
- Total income: W‑2 $84,900 + interest $1,470 + net business $9,150 = $95,520
- Self‑employment tax (approx): 0.153 × 0.9235 × 9,150 ≈ $1,292.50
- Deduction for half SE tax: ≈ $646.25
- Student loan interest deduction: $2,500
- AGI ≈ 95,520 − 646.25 − 2,500 = $92,373.75
- Standard deduction (MFJ): 27,700 → taxable income ≈ 64,673.75
- Estimated federal income tax (2023 brackets) ≈ $7,321
- Add self‑employment tax ≈ $1,292.5 → total tax ≈ $8,613.5

B. Actual home office method (rent allocation only shown; you could add utilities)
- Home office allocation of rent ≈ $4,743 (16.4706% × $28,800)
- Business expenses: $1,350 + $4,743 = $6,093
- Net business profit: 11,200 − 6,093 = $5,107
- Total income: 84,900 + 1,470 + 5,107 = $91,477
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 0.153 × 0.9235 × 5,107 ≈ $721.33
- Deduction half SE tax ≈ $360.66
- Student loan interest deduction: $2,500
- AGI ≈ 91,477 − 360.66 − 2,500 = $88,615.34
- Taxable income ≈ 60,915.34
- Estimated federal income tax ≈ $6,870
- Add self‑employment tax ≈ $721.33 → total tax ≈ $7,591.17

Takeaway from the two calculations: using the actual method (and documenting rent/utilities) looks significantly more favorable in your situation — roughly $1,000 less total tax in this estimate. The exact change will depend on any additional business expenses you can document, withholding, credits, and state tax adjustments.

4) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — e.g., student loan interest, half SE tax adjustment)
- Schedule C (report self‑employment income and business expenses)
- Schedule SE (calculate self‑employment tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you choose actual home‑office method
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize; otherwise not used
- Schedule B — only if total interest/dividends > $1,500 (your amounts are currently < $1,500 combined)
- Form 1040‑ES — if you need to make estimated tax payments for self‑employment income
- Oregon state return: Form OR‑40 (resident). Oregon begins with federal AGI and has state adjustments — check Oregon instructions or a tax preparer for state specifics.

5) Practical recommendations
- Decide home‑office method. Given your $2,400/month rent, the actual method will likely give a larger deduction if you can substantiate the allocation (rent receipts, utility bills, floor plan, photos, lease). If you prefer simplicity and lower audit paperwork, use the simplified method ($700).
- Ensure the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are clearly business expenses (keep receipts). If they are employee unreimbursed costs, they are not deductible.
- Keep all receipts and records: 1099s, 1098‑E, charity receipt, rent and utility bills, business receipts, logs showing exclusive use of the home office.
- Make estimated federal (and state) tax payments if you expect liability after withholding. Use Form 1040‑ES for federal quarterly payments.
- File Oregon Form OR‑40; Oregon taxes start from federal AGI but there are state adjustments—consider state treatment of student loan interest and other items (check OR instructions or a tax preparer).
- If you want, I can: (a) draft a checklist of the exact documents to collect, (b) compute your tax in more detail if you provide withholding amounts from your W‑2 and any 1099s, or (c) prepare a draft Schedule C / Form 8829 calculation using your utility numbers.

Disclaimer
This is general guidance based on the information you provided and U.S. federal tax rules through mid‑2024. It is not formal tax advice. For complex questions or to finalize your return, I recommend confirming with a CPA or tax preparer (especially for state specifics and to ensure you choose the optimal home‑office method and correctly report all deductions).

