Short answer up front
- You will generally file Form 1040 (Married Filing Jointly) and use Schedule C (self‑employment) + Schedule SE (self‑employment tax). 
- Take the regular home‑office deduction (Form 8829) if you can substantiate expenses — it looks better than the simplified method in your case. 
- Use the standard deduction ($27,700 for MFJ in 2023); your small itemized deductions are unlikely to exceed it.
- Student loan interest is likely fully deductible (up to $2,500, subject to MAGI limits), and your business expenses should be claimed on Schedule C (not Schedule A).

Detailed walkthrough and calculations

1) Calculate gross income (tax year 2023)
- W‑2 wages (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900
- Self‑employment (web design): $11,200 — report on Schedule C
- Interest (Goldman Marcus): $920 — report on Form 1040 interest line (attach 1099‑INT if issued)
- TD Ameritrade: $550 — treat as interest/dividends if that’s what it is; if it’s proceeds from sales (1099‑B) different rules apply. Clarify what type it is.

Total gross income (example): 84,900 + 11,200 + 920 + 550 = $97,570

2) Business (self‑employment) items
- Report the 11,200 web‑design income on Schedule C.
- Business expenses: you said $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses.” If those are expenses for your web‑design business (software, supplies, internet portion, client costs), they belong on Schedule C. If the $1,350 is unreimbursed employee expenses from an employer job, those are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Home office: you stated 140 sq ft exclusive use in an 850 sq ft home → office percentage = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%.

Home‑office choice and recommended method:
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 ft = $700 deduction on Schedule C.
- Regular method: use Form 8829 to take 16.47% of allocable home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, repairs attributable to the office, etc.). With rent $2,400/mo = $28,800/yr, the office share of rent = 28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,743 (plus any allocable utilities etc.). Because $4,743 >> $700, the regular method almost certainly gives you a larger deduction if you only have rent and possibly utilities to allocate. You must be able to substantiate exclusive and regular use and keep records/receipts.

Effect on self‑employment tax and AGI (illustrative)
- Using regular method and assuming only the $1,350 business expenses plus the office share of rent:
  - Net self‑employment income ≈ 11,200 − 4,743 − 1,350 = $5,107
  - Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% × 5,107 ≈ $725
  - Deductible half of SE tax (above‑the‑line on Schedule 1) ≈ $362.50

- Using simplified method (for comparison):
  - Net SE income ≈ 11,200 − 700 − 1,350 = $9,150
  - SE tax ≈ ~$1,298 (substantially higher)

So regular method looks better here.

3) Student loan interest
- You paid $2,700 to FedLoan (2018–2019 loans). The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (per return) for 2023. It is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1).
- The deduction phases out at higher MAGI for MFJ; based on the income numbers above your MAGI will be well below the phaseout threshold, so you should be able to take the full $2,500 (unless there are other large income items you haven’t mentioned). I can check phaseout with exact MAGI if you want.

4) Charitable contribution
- Cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank: $1,350 — deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. Because your likely itemized deductions appear small, you will probably take the standard deduction instead (see below). Keep the donation receipt.

5) Standard deduction vs itemize
- Standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly in 2023 = $27,700.
- Your itemizable deductions you mentioned: charitable $1,350; there’s no mortgage interest or large state/local taxes mentioned. So itemizing is unlikely to exceed $27,700. Therefore take the standard deduction.

6) Forms you will use (minimum/typical)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — adjustments to income (half of SE tax, student loan interest deduction).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web‑design income and ordinary business expenses.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular method for the home office; the resulting amount flows to Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form(s) 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B — include whatever statements Goldman and TD Ameritrade issue (report interest and dividends). W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics goes with Form 1040.
- Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (not recommended with the amounts shown).

7) Estimated tax / withholdings
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net SE income (Schedule SE). You should check how much federal tax was withheld on the W‑2 and whether you need to make estimated tax payments for self‑employment income to avoid underpayment penalties. If you want, I can estimate federal tax due/refund if you provide total W‑2 federal withholding.

8) Recordkeeping and documentation
- Keep receipts for the home‑office allocation, rent statements, utility bills, home‑office photos/floorplan, receipts for that $1,350 donation, receipts for business expenses, and 1099s/W‑2s.
- If you claim the regular home‑office method, keep support for all allocated home expenses.

9) Oregon state return
- You will also file Oregon state return (Form OR‑40). Oregon tax rules differ in detail; report your federal AGI as a starting point and check for Oregon additions/subtractions. If you want, I can help estimate Oregon tax.

Questions I have for you / recommended next steps
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web design work? (If yes, they go on Schedule C. If they are employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible federally for 2018–2025.)
2. Did you receive any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC or 1099‑B from TD Ameritrade (i.e., were there sales/capital gains)? Or are the $550 and $920 purely interest/dividends reported on 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV?
3. How much federal income tax was withheld on your W‑2? (I can estimate whether you will owe tax or get a refund.)
4. Do you want me to run precise calculations and a draft return estimate (I can compute taxable income, federal tax, self‑employment tax, and likely refund/amount due) using the regular home‑office method?

If you confirm the items above (especially whether the $1,350 expenses are business expenses and the nature of the TD Ameritrade amount), I’ll give a precise, line‑by‑line estimate and list the exact numbers to enter on each form.

