- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350 (need clarification — see below)

Clarifying question (important)
- Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web design work (supplies, software, hosting, client travel, etc.)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job? (If they’re employee unreimbursed expenses they are not deductible on a federal return for 2018–2025.)

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment income (Schedule C): Your web design gross income ($11,200) minus ordinary and necessary business expenses. Business expenses can include supplies, software, hosting, phone, business-related portions of internet, and the home office allocation (see below). If the $1,350 in “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the web design, include them on Schedule C.
- Home office deduction (self‑employed only): Allowed because you stated the space (140 sq ft) is used exclusively for the business. Two methods:
  - Regular (actual-expense) method: prorate actual indirect expenses by % of home used for business: 140/850 ≈ 16.47%. For rent of $28,800/year, that prorated portion is about $28,800 × 0.1647 ≈ $4,742. You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the regular method.
  - Simplified method: $5 × square feet = $5 × 140 = $700 (no Form 8829 required; report on Schedule C).
  - Recommendation: the regular method will likely give a larger deduction here (roughly $4.7k vs $700). Keep careful records showing exclusive use and the square footage calculation.
- Self‑employment tax deduction: You pay self‑employment (SE) tax on net Schedule C profit and may deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: You paid $2,700 to FedLoan; the maximum federal student loan interest deduction is $2,500 (above‑the‑line) assuming your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) is under the phaseout threshold (for 2023 MFJ the MAGI phaseout is $145,000–$175,000). Because your total income looks well under that range, you should be able to claim the $2,500 limit. Use Form 1098‑E from your servicer as backup.
- Charitable gift: $1,350 cash gift is deductible if you itemize (with your receipt). If you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct this on your federal return (no special universal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023).

What you likely should do: standard deduction vs. itemize
- Standard deduction for 2023, Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your likely federal itemizable deductions based on the numbers you gave:
  - Charitable cash: $1,350
  - (No mortgage interest or property tax listed; SALT deduction for state/local tax limited to $10,000 if you had it.)
  => Itemized total will be far less than $27,700.
- Recommendation: Take the standard deduction for federal purposes. It’s almost certainly better unless you have other itemizable items you didn’t list (mortgage interest, significant state income/property taxes, large medical expenses, etc.).

How the home office affects things
- The home office deduction only applies to self‑employed income (Schedule C). It reduces Schedule C net profit and therefore reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- If you use the regular method, compute and claim it on Form 8829 and carry the allowed amount to Schedule C.
- If you use the simplified method, multiply sq ft × $5 and enter that on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- You cannot use the home office deduction to offset W‑2 income.

Estimated numbers (example) — assuming the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses ARE business expenses
- Self‑employment gross: $11,200
- Business expenses (example): $1,350 (other business expenses) + home office regular allocation ≈ $4,742 = total business expenses ≈ $6,092
- Schedule C net profit ≈ $11,200 − $6,092 = $5,108
- Self‑employment tax (approx): 15.3% on 92.35% of net = ≈ $721; half deductible ≈ $360
- Student loan interest deduction allowed (limited to $2,500)
- Total taxable income estimate: W‑2 $84,900 + net Schedule C $5,108 + interest $1,470 = $91,478; minus half SE tax ($360) and minus student loan interest ($2,500) → AGI ≈ $88,618; minus standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $60,918.
- Bottom line: the home office and business expenses materially reduce your self‑employment taxable income and SE tax. If instead you used the simplified home office ($700) the tax savings would be smaller.

If the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are EMPLOYEE expenses for your W‑2 job
- Those are not deductible on the federal return through 2025 (the miscellaneous itemized deduction for unreimbursed employee expenses is suspended). They would NOT be deductible unless you are in a specific category (armed forces reservist, qualified performing artist, fee-basis government official, etc.). Tell me if you fall into one of those categories.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (individual return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — used for some adjustments (student loan interest is shown here) and reporting other income
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular home‑office method; otherwise use simplified method and no 8829
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) — use it to compute and support the student loan interest deduction
- 1099‑INTs (from Goldman Sachs and TD Ameritrade) — report interest income; Schedule B only required if total interest + dividend > $1,500 (you are under that threshold)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you decide to itemize (but likely not beneficial here)
- State return: Oregon Form OR‑40 (or other Oregon filing form). State rules and deductions differ from federal; you’ll need to report federal AGI and follow Oregon instructions. I can pull specific Oregon guidance if you want.

Recordkeeping you should keep
- Home office: floor plan/square footage calculation, lease, cancelled rent checks or bank statements showing rent paid, utility bills, and any direct expense receipts
- Business receipts/invoices for the web design work, including the $1,350 business expenses if those are business-related
- 1098‑E for student loan interest, and charity receipt for the $1,350 donation
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑NEC or other 1099s you receive
- Logs or documentation for any other business expenses (mileage, phone bills if allocated to business, software subscriptions, etc.)

Other considerations
- Estimated taxes / withholding: you will owe self‑employment tax and some additional income tax on the Schedule C profit. If you have little or no tax withholding covering these amounts, consider increasing W‑4 withholding or making quarterly estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Oregon state tax: state treatment of deductions and credits differs; charitable contributions and student loan interest have different state treatments — I can check Oregon specifics if you want.
- If your self‑employment activity is small and you want simpler recordkeeping, the simplified home‑office method is easier but probably gives a smaller deduction here.

Next steps / offer
