Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which forms you’ll need. I have a couple of short clarifying questions at the end so I can make any calculations more exact.

Quick summary / bottom line
- For federal 2023 you’ll likely be better off taking the standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) unless you have large additional itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, or more charitable gifts).
- You can (and should) report your web‑design income and business expenses on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home office deduction is available only if the web design work is self‑employment (not for a W‑2 job).
- Student loan interest: you can claim up to $2,500 as an above‑the‑line deduction if your MAGI is below the phaseout (MFJ phaseout starts at $145,000 and ends at $175,000). Based on the incomes you listed, you should qualify for the full $2,500 (you paid $2,700 so the deductible cap is $2,500).
- Charitable gift of $1,350 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not get that federal deduction.
- You will need Form 1040 plus Schedule C (profit/loss from business), Schedule SE (self‑employment tax), Schedule 1 for adjustments (student loan interest, half SE tax), and Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) if you claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) 20% deduction. For Oregon, file the resident return (OR‑40) and include the federal numbers.

Details

1) Business (web design) income — how to report and what you can deduct
- Report the $11,200 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them — but you report all income even if you didn’t get a 1099.
- Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. That includes your unreimbursed business expenses ($1,350) if they were for the web design work (software, supplies, internet portion, client-related travel, subscriptions, etc.). If those unreimbursed expenses were for your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible on your federal return (suspended through 2025).
- Self‑employment tax: net profit from Schedule C is subject to SE tax. You’ll calculate SE tax on Schedule SE and you get to deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

2) Home office
- You said the office is 140 sq ft exclusive and your home is 850 sq ft. Percentage = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 140 sq ft = $700 (simple, no depreciation or separate records).
  - Actual expense method: allocate a share (≈16.47%) of allowable home expenses to the business. For example, your annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800; allocated portion ≈ $4,745. You may also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., to increase the deduction. (If you own, depreciation and mortgage interest rules apply.)
- Use the actual method if your share of rent + utilities etc. gives a larger deduction than the $700 simplified amount. The home‑office deduction is taken on Schedule C and reduces your business net profit (and thus income subject to income tax and SE tax).
- Important: the home office deduction is only allowed if the space is used regularly and exclusively for the business and only by you for that business. It is not available for an employee’s unreimbursed home office.

3) Student loan interest ($2,700)
- The maximum allowable deduction is $2,500 in 2023. The deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (reported on Schedule 1).
- Phaseout for married filing jointly: MAGI between $145,000 and $175,000 reduces or eliminates the deduction. Based on the income numbers you gave (W‑2 $84,900 + self‑employment net income + interest income), your MAGI is likely well under $145k, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500. (I’ll confirm exact MAGI if you want a precise calculation.)
- You must be legally obligated on the loan and you should receive Form 1098‑E from the servicer showing interest paid in 2023. Keep that and your loan statements.

4) Interest income ($920 + $550 = $1,470)
- Report interest on Form 1040. If total taxable interest is more than $1,500 you would attach Schedule B; your total is just under that threshold so Schedule B not required.

5) Charitable donation ($1,350)
- Deductible on Schedule A if you itemize. You must keep the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot claim this on the federal return.

6) Standard deduction vs itemizing
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your known itemizable item (charitable) = $1,350. You would need significant additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, property taxes, state/local taxes up to $10,000 cap, large medical expenses exceeding the floor, etc.) to exceed $27,700 and justify itemizing.
- Based on the items you listed, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction on the federal return.

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- If you have net qualified business income from the Schedule C activity, you may be able to claim up to 20% of QBI (Form 8995) as a deduction. Because your total taxable income appears far below the 2023 thresholds for MFJ, you should be eligible for the simple QBI calculation. This is reported on Form 1040 (via Form 8995). It reduces taxable income but is not an itemized deduction.

8) State (Oregon)
- You are an Oregon resident and must file OR‑40 (Oregon individual income tax return). Oregon starts from federal taxable income with adjustments; some federal adjustments/deductions differ at the state level. If you made estimated tax payments for federal/state, track those.

Forms you will likely need
- Federal:
  - Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
  - Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction and adjustments
  - Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — web design income and business expenses, including home office
  - Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
  - Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for QBI deduction (likely Form 8995 if under thresholds)
  - Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you choose to itemize (probably you won’t)
  - Schedule B only if interest/dividend totals exceed $1,500 (not in your case)
- State:
  - Oregon Form OR‑40 (Resident) plus any supporting schedules

Documentation to gather
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for web‑design clients (if any)
- Records/receipts for the $1,350 business expenses and the $1,350 charitable contribution (receipt)
- 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) or loan statements showing interest paid in 2023
- 1099‑INT from Goldman Sachs Marcus and TD Ameritrade
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills for actual home office calculation
- Any state tax payments, property tax statements, mortgage interest statements, etc., if applicable

Practical next steps / questions for me
1. Were the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses for your web‑design business (self‑employment) or for your W‑2 job? If they’re business expenses, they belong on Schedule C; if they’re W‑2 employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible federally.
2. Confirm the $2,700 is interest you actually paid in 2023 and that you (or you and your spouse) are the borrower(s). Do you have Form 1098‑E from FedLoan showing that amount?
