How each item is treated
- Web‑design income ($11,200): Report on Schedule C (Form 1040). You may deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses (software, supplies, client travel, advertising, portions of home office, etc.) on Schedule C. If net profit is $400 or more you must pay self‑employment tax and file Schedule SE.

- Interest / brokerage ($1,470): Report on Form 1040. Banks/brokers should send you 1099‑INT / consolidated 1099. You only need Schedule B if total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500; your total is $1,470 so Schedule B is not required but you still report the amounts on your 1040.

- Home office (140 sq ft exclusive; 850 sq ft total): You have two choices:
  1. Simplified method: $5.00 × 140 sq ft = $700 deduction (no Form 8829). Claim on Schedule C.
  2. Actual‑expense method: Deduct the business percentage of actual home expenses. Business percentage = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%. For rent alone: 16.47% × $28,800 = $4,745 (plus a prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, etc.). If you choose actual expenses and you’re self‑employed, you normally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
  Notes:
   - The home‑office deduction is available only for the self‑employment business, not for W‑2 employment.
   - The deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business. Any excess home‑office expense may be carried forward (rules differ for passive activity vs nonpassive; generally for self‑employed active business you can carry forward).

- Student loan interest ($2,700 paid): The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (you claim it on Schedule 1). Maximum deduction is $2,500 (so you can only deduct up to $2,500 of your $2,700). There is an income phaseout for MFJ in 2023: MAGI begins phasing out at $145,000 and is fully phased out at $175,000. Based on the numbers you provided, your MAGI will be well under that, so you should be able to take the full $2,500 (assuming the loan is qualified and you meet other rules).

- Charitable gift ($1,350): This is an itemized deduction. For 2023 the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $27,700. Unless your total itemized deductions (state & local taxes up to $10k, mortgage interest, charitable gifts, medical above threshold, etc.) exceed $27,700, you should take the standard deduction. With only $1,350 of charitable gifts shown and no mortgage interest supplied, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.

- Unreimbursed employee expenses: If these $1,350 items are unreimbursed costs related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. If they are business expenses for the web design work, report them on Schedule C and they reduce your business profit.

What forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — to report adjustments such as student loan interest deduction and the half of self‑employment tax deduction
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the web‑design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax (Social Security & Medicare) on net SE earnings
