2) Business expenses and “unreimbursed expenses”
- If the $1,350 of unreimbursed expenses are for the web‑design business (supplies, software, advertising, travel, etc.), claim them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible on your federal return for 2018–2025.
- Keep receipts and documentation for all business expenses.

3) Home‑office deduction
- Exclusive / regular use: you meet that requirement (140 sq ft exclusive).
- Two methods:
  - Simplified: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700. Entry is on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
  - Actual: business-use percentage = 140 / 850 = 16.4706%. Rent $28,800 × 16.4706% ≈ $4,744. You can also allocate utilities, renters insurance, repairs proportionally if you have them. Use Form 8829 to compute if you choose actual.
- Note: Actual method deductions for business use of home cannot exceed net profit from that business for the year; excess may be carried forward (see Form 8829 instructions). Simplified method has no carryforward but gives a smaller deduction here.

Example comparison (approximate)
- Option A — simplified home office and $1,350 business expenses:
  - Schedule C expenses: $700 + $1,350 = $2,050 → net SE income = $11,200 − $2,050 = $9,150.
  - Self‑employment tax (approx): net earnings × 0.9235 × 15.3% ≈ $1,293 → half deductible ≈ $646.
  - Student loan interest deduction (max) = $2,500 (you paid $2,700 but limit is $2,500).
  - These adjustments reduce your AGI; then you’d almost certainly take the standard deduction $27,700.
- Option B — actual home office (rent allocation) + $1,350 business expenses:
  - Schedule C expenses: $4,744 + $1,350 = $6,094 → net SE income ≈ $5,106.
  - SE tax ≈ $720 → half deductible ≈ $360.
  - Student loan interest deduction still capped at $2,500.
- Which method is better? It depends — actual gives a larger direct business expense but reduces the SE tax deduction. Run both calculations (or let your tax software) to see which produces the smallest total tax. With the numbers above, simplified vs actual produce similar total taxable income; you should compute both precisely before filing.

4) Student‑loan interest
- Deduction up to $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1) if you meet eligibility (you’re legally obligated to pay, not claimed by someone else, and your MAGI is below the phaseout).
- Phaseout for Married Filing Jointly in 2023: deduction phases out between MAGI of $145,000 and $175,000. With your income mix (~$97,570 gross before adjustments) you are well below the phaseout, so you should get the full $2,500 cap (even though you paid $2,700).

5) Charitable contributions
- $1,350 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). With only $1,350 of charity and no other large itemized deductions provided, the standard deduction $27,700 will almost certainly be larger. If you do have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to $10,000 SALT cap, large medical expenses >7.5% AGI, etc.), add them up and compare to $27,700.

6) Self‑employment tax and withholding
- Your Schedule C net income is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You may owe additional tax beyond what is withheld on your W‑2. Because you have substantial W‑2 wages, part of your federal tax may already be covered by W‑2 withholding, but SE tax (and any extra income tax) can create a balance due — check withholding or make estimated tax payments when necessary.

7) Oregon state return
- You must file Oregon state tax return (resident) — use Form OR‑40 (or the appropriate OR form). Oregon rules differ for some adjustments/deductions; charitable gifts and certain federal adjustments may be treated differently. State taxable income calculation follows OR rules; consult Oregon Dept. of Revenue guidance or tax software.

8) Forms you will use (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest and the deduction for half of self‑employment tax
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report $11,200 and business expenses (home‑office deduction, $1,350 if business related, etc.)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
