Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer / recommendation
- You will almost certainly take the 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly ($27,700) — your itemizable deductions (charity $1,350 plus any state/local taxes or mortgage interest you didn’t mention) are far below that.
- You can take the student loan interest adjustment (up to $2,500) on your 2023 federal return (assuming the $2,700 is interest paid in 2023). That reduces your AGI even if you take the standard deduction.
- Your web‑design income ($11,200) is reported on Schedule C; you can deduct business expenses there (including a home‑office deduction). Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are NOT deductible for 2018–2025, but business expenses for the self‑employment work are deductible.
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net profit from the web‑design work (use Schedule SE), and you may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995) — but that’s separate from the standard/itemized decision.

Details and calculations (useful numbers)
1) Standard vs itemize
- 2023 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly): $27,700.
- Your known itemizable amount: charitable contributions $1,350. That is much smaller than $27,700, so standard deduction is the better choice unless you have substantial additional deductible items (large mortgage interest, property tax, large unreimbursed medical expenses above the floor, or large state/local tax up to the $10,000 SALT cap).

2) Student loan interest
- Up to $2,500 is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1 to Form 1040). You reported $2,700 paid — the maximum deductible is $2,500 for 2023.
- Phaseout applies by MAGI for MFJ, but with your income (~$97,600 total reported below) you are under the phaseout range and should get the full $2,500 (assuming the $2,700 was paid in 2023). You should receive Form 1098‑E from the servicer (but you can still claim the deduction with records even if a 1098‑E wasn’t issued).

3) Charity
- $1,350 cash gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank is an itemized deduction (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction you cannot deduct this on your federal return.

4) Unreimbursed expenses
- If these $1,350 are expenses you paid as an employee for your W‑2 job (unreimbursed employee business expenses), they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025. Do not include them on Schedule A.
- If instead the $1,350 were business expenses for your web‑design clients (self‑employment expenses), report them on Schedule C and they will reduce your self‑employment net profit. Please confirm which they are.

5) Home office (self‑employed web design)
You can take a home‑office deduction only for business use connected to your self‑employment income (Schedule C). You cannot take it for W‑2 employment.

Two methods:
A. Simplified method
- $5.00 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft: 140 × $5 = $700 deduction (no depreciation).

B. Regular (actual) method
- Business percentage = 140 sq ft / 850 sq ft = 16.47% (approx).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800. Rent portion allocable to the home office ≈ 16.47% × $28,800 ≈ $4,745.
- With the actual method you can also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., and you may take depreciation on the business portion where applicable (Form 8829 / Form 4562 as appropriate).
- The home‑office deduction cannot exceed business income from that activity for the year (loss rules and passive/at‑risk rules can limit it).

Which method to pick
- If you have few other home‑related business expenses, the simplified method is simpler (no Form 8829). If you have large rent allocation or many other home expenses, actual method may give a larger deduction (as shown above, actual allocation of rent alone produces a bigger deduction than simplified).
- However, the deduction is limited by your Schedule C net profit; don’t assume you can create or increase a business loss beyond allowed limits without consequences.

6) Self‑employment tax and other effects
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net earnings) reported on Schedule SE. You get an above‑the‑line deduction for half of self‑employment tax (Schedule 1).
- Example (very rough): if you choose actual home‑office rent allocation ($4,745) + business expenses $1,350, total expenses ≈ $6,095 → net profit ≈ $11,200 − $6,095 = $5,105. Self‑employment tax would be computed on ~92.35% of $5,105; half of that SE tax is deductible (reduces AGI).

7) Interest and brokerage income
- Marcus bank interest ($920) and TD Ameritrade ($550) should be reported on Form 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV/1099‑B depending on type. Include these on your Form 1040 (interest and investment income lines). If there are capital gains from TD Ameritrade/brokerage sales, you'll need Form 8949/Schedule D as applicable.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (2023)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to income: student loan interest, half of SE tax, etc.)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report web design income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use the actual (regular) method to calculate the home‑office deduction (alternative: use the simplified method and report home office on Schedule C without 8829)
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) only if you decide to itemize (likely not)
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the Qualified Business Income deduction, if applicable
- Supporting forms you should get and keep: Form 1098‑E (student loan interest), Form(s) 1099‑INT, 1099‑NEC (if clients issued them), 1099‑B (brokerage), receipts for charitable gifts and business expenses.

State taxes (Oregon)
- File Oregon state return (OR‑40 for residents) — Oregon generally starts with your federal AGI. State treatment of some items may differ, so keep records and forms used for federal filing. Oregon also taxes interest and self‑employment income.

Practical next steps / questions for you
1. Tell me whether the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are for your web‑design business or for your W‑2 job.
2. Confirm the $2,700 of student‑loan interest was paid in 2023 and whether you received Form 1098‑E.
3. Do you want me to run quick sample math showing:
   - (A) simplified home‑office method and final taxable income estimate, and
   - (B) actual method using rent allocation,
   so you can see which home‑office method is more beneficial?
4. Do you have any other deductions or documents (mortgage interest, property tax, state tax paid, large medical expenses, 1099‑NEC receipts)?

Notes / cautions
- This is general guidance — for complex tax planning, state-specific issues, or if you want to minimize audit risk while optimizing deductions, consider a licensed tax preparer or CPA.
- Keep receipts and documentation for the home‑office measurement, rent payments, charitable receipts, and business expense records in case of questions.

If you want, I can:
- compute estimated federal tax and self‑employment tax for the scenarios above, or
- draft a checklist of documents to give to a tax preparer, or
- prepare suggested entries to enter into tax software step‑by‑step. Which would you like next?

