- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive use; home = 850 sq ft; rent $2,400/mo = $28,800/yr
- Student loan interest paid: $2,700 (FedLoan)
- Charitable cash gift: $1,350 (receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $1,350 (please confirm whether these are business expenses for your web design or unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job)

1) Standard deduction vs. itemize (federal)
- 2023 standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your documented itemizable deduction shown here is the $1,350 charitable gift. That is far below the standard deduction, so you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction unless you have other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large medical expenses, significant state/local taxes paid, etc.) that you didn’t list.
- Note: student loan interest is an “above-the-line” adjustment to income (Schedule 1) and reduces your AGI whether or not you itemize.

Recommendation: take the standard deduction (unless you actually have much larger itemizable deductions not listed).

2) Student loan interest
- Maximum federal deduction = $2,500 per return (if you qualify).
- There is an income phaseout for married filing jointly in 2023: the deduction phases out for MAGI between $145,000 and $175,000; if MAGI ≥ $175,000, you cannot take it.
- Based on the income you listed (W-2 + net self‑employment roughly in the mid‑$90k area), you are likely well below the $145k phaseout floor and therefore should be able to deduct up to the $2,500 limit. Because you paid $2,700, the deductible amount would be capped at $2,500.
- You’ll need Form 1098-E (student loan interest statement) or your loan statement to substantiate the amount paid.

3) Self‑employment (web design) — what to report and self-employment tax
- Report the web design income on Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business).
- Any ordinary and necessary business expenses related to the web design (hosting, software, supplies, phone portion, client-related travel, etc.) should be deducted on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for web design, include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job, they are NOT deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025.
- If net profit from Schedule C is $400 or more, you must file Schedule SE and pay self‑employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare). You also get to deduct one-half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — generally 20% of qualified business income for many small-service businesses if you meet taxable income requirements. With your income level, you will likely be eligible for the simplified QBI deduction; complete Form 8995 (or 8995-A if needed) to calculate it.

4) Home office treatment
- Because you stated the space is used exclusively for business (140 sq ft exclusively), a home office deduction is likely allowed for the portion of your home used regularly and exclusively for the web design business.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 140 sq ft = $700. Simple to use and common for small businesses.
  - Regular (actual) method: claim the business percentage of actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, HOA, repairs allocable to the office, etc.). Your business percentage = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%. On rent alone: $28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,744. Add allocable utilities, etc., and the total could be substantially larger.
- Important limits:
  - The home office deduction (both methods) cannot exceed the net profit from the business (it is limited to the business income after other business expenses). If your net business income is small, a large regular-method home office deduction may be limited.
  - The simplified method is typically limited by the business profit as well, but it is easier to calculate and often preferable when gross business income is modest.
- Recommendation: Use the simplified method ($700) unless you expect the actual-method allocation (including rent share) to be larger and not limited by your net profit. Because your gross web design income is $11,200, and you have other business expenses, the simplified method will often be the safe, low-burden choice.

5) Interest income reporting
- Total interest = $1,470. Report this on Form 1040 (interest line). Schedule B is required only if your taxable interest + dividends exceed $1,500. Since you are just under $1,500, Schedule B is not required; still report the interest on Form 1040.

6) Unreimbursed expenses — clarify
- If the $1,350 is business expenses for the web design work: include them on Schedule C.
- If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W-2 job: not deductible on federal returns (through 2025), so they would not help you. (Oregon state rules may differ — check OR rules.)

7) Charitable contribution
- $1,350 cash gift with receipt — deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because the standard deduction is higher, you would only get this benefit if your total itemized deductions exceed $27,700 (unlikely based on the items you listed).

