Quick facts / assumptions I use below
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (2023). Federal standard deduction for MFJ (2023) = $27,700.
- Income you gave: W‑2 wages $84,900; web‑design self‑employment $11,200; interest/ brokerage $920 + $550 = $1,470.
- Home: renting at $2,400/month → $28,800/year; home office = 140 sq ft exclusive of an 850 sq ft home → office % = 140 / 850 = 16.47%.
- Student loan interest paid = $2,700 (deduction capped at $2,500).
- Charitable cash gift = $1,350 (you have receipt).
- You also listed $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” — I need to confirm whether these are business expenses for the web‑design work or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job. (If they are employee unreimbursed expenses, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law; if they are business expenses, they are deductible on Schedule C.)

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment (web design):
  - Report the $11,200 on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business).
  - Deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C. If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the web work, include them on Schedule C.
  - Home office: deductible on Schedule C either by the simplified method or the actual expense method (see below).
  - You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income; calculate it on Schedule SE. You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
  - You will likely be eligible for the 20% Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995) because your taxable income is well below the 2023 phase‑out threshold — this gives up to 20% of qualified business income (QBI) as a deduction on Form 1040.

- Student loan interest:
  - You can deduct up to $2,500 of student loan interest as an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1) if your MAGI is below the phaseout range. For MFJ in 2023, the phaseout begins at $145,000 and phases out completely at $175,000. Based on incomes you gave, you’re well under the phaseout, so you can take the full $2,500 (cap) of your $2,700 paid.

- Charitable contribution:
  - The $1,350 cash gift to St. Mary’s is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). It’s unlikely to push total itemized deductions above the standard deduction unless you have significant state/local taxes, mortgage interest, high medical expenses, or other itemizable amounts.

- Unreimbursed employee expenses:
  - If those $1,350 are personal unreimbursed expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act).

Home‑office treatment — simplified vs actual
- Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet used for business (max 300 sq ft).
  - For 140 sq ft → deduction = 140 × $5 = $700.
  - Easy: no Form 8829. Enter on Schedule C.

- Actual expense method:
  - Allocate a percentage of your home expenses to the business (your office % = 16.47%).
  - With rent $28,800/year → home‑office share = 16.47% × $28,800 ≈ $4,744.94.
  - If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, those are prorated too. Use Form 8829 to compute and carry to Schedule C.
  - Actual method usually gives a larger deduction here because your rent is high relative to the simplified amount — BUT you must meet the exclusive and regular use rules (you said the 140 sq ft is exclusive).

Which home‑office method to use?
- Based on rent alone, the actual method gives a much larger deduction (~$4,745 vs $700). If you have supporting docs and the space is exclusively used for business, the actual method is usually better here. Use Form 8829 to compute the allowable deduction and show prorated amounts.

Example tax impact (illustrative; round numbers)
I’ll show 2 quick scenarios to illustrate the effect. I assume the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are business expenses for the web design work. If they are employee expenses, ignore them in Schedule C and the numbers change.

Scenario A — actual home‑office (office share of rent = $4,744.94)
- Self‑employment gross = $11,200
- Business expenses = $1,350 (other) + $4,744.94 (home office) = $6,094.94
- Net self‑employment income = $11,200 − $6,094.94 = $5,105.06
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × (92.35% of net) ≈ $720.8 → half deductible ≈ $360.4
- QBI (20% of net) ≈ $1,021
- Federal AGI ≈ W‑2 84,900 + interest 1,470 + net SE 5,105.06 = 91,475.06
- Subtract above‑the‑line: student loan interest $2,500 + half SE tax $360.4 = $2,860.4 → AGI ≈ $88,614.7
- Subtract standard deduction $27,700 → taxable income ≈ $60,915
- After QBI (taken as deduction) taxable income ≈ $59,894 (approximate)

Scenario B — simplified home‑office ($700)  
- Business expenses = $1,350 + $700 = $2,050
- Net self‑employment income = $11,200 − $2,050 = $9,150
- Self‑employment tax ≈ $1,292.9 → half deductible ≈ $646.4
- QBI ≈ 20% × 9,150 = $1,830
- Federal AGI ≈ 84,900 + 1,470 + 9,150 = 95,520
- Subtract above‑the‑line: student loan $2,500 + half SE tax $646.4 = $3,146.4 → AGI ≈ $92,373.6
- Subtract standard deduction $27,700 → taxable ≈ $64,674
- After QBI deduction taxable ≈ $62,844 (approximate)

Bottom line from examples: the actual method yields a noticeably lower taxable income in your case. These are approximations to show relative impact — the exact amounts depend on whether that $1,350 of “unreimbursed expenses” is business‑related and whether you have other deductible business costs.

Should you itemize?
- Very likely: No — take the standard deduction ($27,700) for 2023. With only $1,350 in charitable gifts and no large mortgage interest or large state/local taxes listed, your itemized total is probably well below the standard deduction.
- If you paid large Oregon state income taxes or property taxes this year, recheck — if your total itemized deductions > $27,700, itemize (Schedule A). Note SALT deduction is capped at $10,000.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for reporting some adjustments (student loan interest is reported as adjustment)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and business expenses, including the home office amount (if simplified) or carryover from Form 8829 (if using actual).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual expense method.
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax and the deductible half.
- Form 8995 (Qualified Business Income Deduction) — likely the simple Form 8995 for QBI (if your taxable income is below the threshold).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (for the $1,350 charitable deduction and any other itemizable amounts).
- Keep W‑2, 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (for self‑employment), 1099‑INT (bank interest), 1099‑DIV or 1099‑B (brokerage statements), and Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) or documentation of interest paid.

Oregon state return
- File Oregon resident return (Form OR‑40) for 2023. Oregon starts with federal AGI and then has state adjustments; include the federal-reporting amounts (wages, business profit/loss, etc.). Oregon has its own standard/itemized rules — generally you’ll use the federal figures as the starting point. If you paid Oregon income tax or had withholding, include that on the state return. If you want, I can call out Oregon specifics once you provide state withholding or other state adjustments.

Records to keep
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics.
- 1099s (NEC, INT, DIV) and brokerage year‑end statements.
- 1098‑E or other proof of student loan interest paid (your loan servicer should send 1098‑E).
- Donation receipt from St. Mary’s (you said you have it) — keep it for substantiation.
- Receipts, invoices, bank statements for the web‑design business expenses.
- Lease statements / rent receipts and utility bills to support actual home office allocation.

Questions / next steps
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for your web‑design work, or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? That changes several things.
2. Do you have other deductible items (state taxes paid, mortgage interest, large medical expenses) that might push you toward itemizing?
