Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Here’s a clear, actionable rundown of what you can (and can’t) deduct, how to treat the home office, whether to itemize, and which forms you’ll need. I’ll also show example math so you can see the tax-impact of the home-office method.

Assumptions / facts you gave
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (federal), live in Portland, OR (state taxes separate)  
- W-2 wages: $84,900 (Desert Sun Logistics)  
- Self‑employment (web design): $11,200 (2 clients) — assume this is reported as self‑employment income, not W‑2  
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $550 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,470 total interest  
- Rent: $2,400/month → $28,800/year for an 850 sq ft rental  
- Home office: 140 sq ft, used exclusively for business (so office % = 140 / 850 ≈ 16.47%)  
- Other items: $2,700 student loan interest paid, $1,350 charitable contribution (receipt), $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” (you didn’t say whether those relate to the self‑employment — treated below)

Important high-level points
- Student loan interest deduction: up to $2,500 (above-the-line) if you qualify based on your MAGI. With your income levels, you are well below typical phaseout limits, so expect to be able to take the full $2,500 (but your actual allowable amount can’t exceed $2,500 even if you paid $2,700). You should receive Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer showing interest paid.  
- Charitable gift ($1,350): deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no general federal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2023.  
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job: NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension). If the $1,350 is a business expense related to your web design self‑employment, it is deductible on Schedule C. I’ll treat it as a business expense below. Tell me if it instead relates to your W‑2 job.  
- Interest income ($1,470): below the $1,500 threshold for required Schedule B, so you can just report it on Form 1040; you should get 1099‑INTs from the banks.

Home office: two methods, and which is better here
You qualify for the home‑office deduction if the 140 sq ft area is used regularly and exclusively for the business and is your principal place of business.

Two options:
1) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 140 * $5 = $700 deduction. (Easy; no Form 8829.)  
2) Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by office % (140/850 ≈ 16.4706%). Your main documented expense is rent: $28,800 × 16.4706% ≈ $4,741 allowed as the home‑office portion of rent. (If you have utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., a pro rata portion can be included too.) You claim this via Form 8829 and Schedule C.

Which is better here? With your numbers the regular method yields a much larger deduction ($4,741 vs $700). The regular method is therefore preferable if your rent is the main home expense and you have documentation.

Example calculations — effect on federal taxable income
I’ll show two scenarios: (A) regular home office (recommended) and (B) simplified home office.

Common items:
- Wages: $84,900  
- Interest: $1,470

A. Regular home-office method (use Form 8829)
- Self‑employment gross: $11,200  
- Home-office portion of rent: $28,800 × (140/850) ≈ $4,741  
- Other business expenses (you listed “$1,350 unreimbursed” — assumed business expenses): $1,350  
- Total business expenses = $4,741 + $1,350 = $6,091  
- Net profit (Schedule C) = $11,200 − $6,091 = $5,109

Self‑employment tax:
- Net earnings for SE tax = 5,109 × 92.35% ≈ $4,718  
- SE tax ≈ $4,718 × 15.3% ≈ $722  
- Half SE tax (above-the-line adjustment) ≈ $361

Above-the-line student loan interest deduction:
- You paid $2,700; maximum allowed is $2,500 (assuming you’re under the income phaseout). So use $2,500 (reported on Schedule 1 as adjustment).

Compute AGI and taxable income:
- Total income = 84,900 + 5,109 + 1,470 = $91,479  
- Adjustments = half SE tax $361 + student loan interest $2,500 = $2,861  
- AGI ≈ 91,479 − 2,861 = $88,618  
- Standard deduction (Married filing jointly 2023) = $27,700  
- Taxable income ≈ $88,618 − $27,700 = $60,918

Possible additional deduction — Qualified Business Income (QBI)
- You likely qualify for the 20% QBI deduction on the net qualified business income. Rough estimate: QBI ≈ $5,109 → 20% ≈ $1,022. That would reduce taxable income further (file Form 8995 to compute/claim). QBI calculations have nuances, but at this income level the simple 20% is likely available.

B. Simplified home‑office method ($5/sq ft = $700)
- Business expenses = $1,350 + $700 = $2,050  
- Net profit = $11,200 − $2,050 = $9,150  
- SE tax roughly ≈ $1,293 (half ≈ $647)  
- AGI and taxable income following same steps would be higher than scenario A; simplified yields significantly less tax savings in your case.

Conclusion from the numbers
- Regular method is substantially better for you (regular ≈ $4,741 deduction vs simplified $700).  
- Claiming the regular home‑office deduction will reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax because it reduces your Schedule C net profit.  
- The student loan interest deduction is likely fully available but limited to $2,500.  
- The $1,350 charitable contribution would only lower federal tax if you itemize; since the standard deduction for MFJ in 2023 is $27,700, the $1,350 charitable gift alone will not make itemizing worthwhile unless you have substantial other deductible items (mortgage interest, property tax, state/local taxes up to $10,000, large medical expenses, etc.). With the information you gave, the standard deduction is almost certainly the better choice.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main tax return.  
- Schedule C (Form 1040), Profit or Loss from Business — report your $11,200 and business expenses ($1,350 plus the home-office deduction amount).  
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method). The result moves to Schedule C. (If you use the simplified method, you do NOT use Form 8829; you report the simplified home-office deduction directly on Schedule C.)  
- Schedule SE (Form 1040) — Self‑Employment Tax (compute SE tax and the deductible half).  
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest deduction and half of SE tax flows here).  
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — Qualified Business Income Deduction (if you want to claim the 20% QBI deduction; most small filers use the simpler Form 8995).  
- Schedule A (Form 1040) — only if you decide to itemize (to claim the $1,350 charitable gift and any other itemizable deductions).  
- Keep supporting forms you will receive: W‑2 (Desert Sun Logistics), 1099‑INT(s) from banks, 1098‑E from student loan servicer (interest paid). If you received 1099‑NEC for contract work, include that too.

State (Oregon) notes
- Oregon uses federal AGI as the starting point for the state return but has differences for some items. You’ll prepare an Oregon return after you finish federal. If you take the federal standard deduction that generally simplifies your Oregon return. For specific Oregon adjustments or credits, consult OR instructions or a tax pro if you want state-specific planning.

Recordkeeping & practical tips
- Keep receipts for rent payments, a copy of your lease, photos/sketch of the home office area, utility bills, receipts for the $1,350 business expense, and the charitable receipt. These substantiate the home-office and business deductions.  
- Make sure the “unreimbursed expenses” are truly business expenses for web design (if not, they aren’t deductible as a W‑2 unreimbursed employee expense).  
- If your tax withholding on the W‑2 isn’t covering the added income + SE tax, consider increasing W‑2 withholding or making estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties. With a modest net business profit, your withholding might already cover it — check after preparing the return or use withholding estimator.

If you want, I can:
- Run a more detailed, line‑by‑line calculation using either home‑office method and show expected federal tax due/refund (including QBI), or  
- Draft the text to enter on Schedule C and Form 8829 (if you want to prepare forms before filing), or  
- Tell you exactly where each amount goes on Form 1040/Schedules.

Which of those would you like me to do next?

