--- AGENT RESPONSE (FULL) ---
Length: 6829 chars
Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer (bottom-line recommendation)
- You should almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2023 (married filing jointly = $27,700) — your itemizable deductions (charity $1,350) are well below that.
- You can — and should — claim the home-office deduction for your web‑design work on Schedule C. With rent at $2,400/month and a 140‑sq‑ft exclusive office (140/850 ≈ 16.47%), the actual-expense method will very likely give a bigger deduction than the simplified method.
- Report your web‑design income on Schedule C, pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE), claim the student‑loan interest deduction (up to $2,500, subject to MAGI phaseouts), and consider the 20% qualified business income (QBI) deduction (Form 8995) if eligible.
- Forms you will need: Form 1040, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use the actual home-office method), Schedule 1 (adjustments like student-loan interest and half SE tax), Form 8995 (QBI), and Form 1040 Schedule A only if you choose to itemize (not recommended in your case). Also Oregon state return (OR-40) — state rules can differ.

Details and quick calculations
1) Income summary you gave
- W‑2 (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900
- Self‑employment (web design, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest: Goldman Sachs Marcus $920 + TD Ameritrade $550 = $1,470
Total gross income before adjustments ≈ $97,570

2) Business (self‑employment) treatment
- Report the web‑design income on Schedule C.
- Legitimate business expenses are deductible on Schedule C. You said “$1,350 unreimbursed expenses” — if those were out‑of‑pocket business expenses for the web design work (hosting, software, supplies, client travel, etc.) treat them as Schedule C deductions. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Home‑office deduction: you have a 140 sq ft office used exclusively for business within an 850 sq ft home → business % ≈ 140/850 = 16.47%.

Two ways to compute the home-office deduction:
a) Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700.

b) Actual expense method: allocate allowable home expenses by the business percentage. Example using your rent:
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800
- Rent portion allocable to office ≈ $28,800 × 0.1647 ≈ $4,744
- Plus you could allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business %), etc.

Which to pick? With rent that high, the actual-expense method (with rent portion ≈ $4,744) almost surely gives a larger deduction than the $700 simplified amount. Use Form 8829 to calculate the allowable actual expenses for the home office.

3) Self‑employment tax and above‑the‑line adjustments
- Net self‑employment profit = $11,200 − business expenses − home‑office portion.
Example using actual method and only the $1,350 business expense and rent portion:
- Expenses total ≈ $1,350 + $4,744 = $6,094 → net SE income ≈ $11,200 − $6,094 = $5,106.
- Self‑employment tax ≈ 15.3% × 92.35% of net SE income ≈ 0.153 × (0.9235 × 5,106) ≈ $721.
- Half of the SE tax (≈ $361) is deductible above‑the‑line on Schedule 1.
- That reduces your AGI.

If instead you used the simplified method:
- Net SE income ≈ $11,200 − $1,350 − $700 = $9,150 → SE tax would be larger.

