Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, how to treat the home office, what forms you’ll use, and give a quick numeric estimate so you can see the difference between the simplified and actual home-office methods.

Summary of the raw numbers you gave
- W‑2 wages (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900
- Self‑employment (web design, 2 clients): $11,200
- Interest: Goldman Sachs Marcus $920 + TD Ameritrade $550 = $1,470
- Student loan interest paid (FedLoan): $2,700 (2018–2019 loans — I assume you’re the borrower)
- Cash charitable gift to St. Mary’s Food Bank: $1,350 (you have receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $1,350 — please clarify whether those were out‑of‑pocket business expenses for your web‑design clients (deductible on Schedule C) or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job (not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law)
- Home: rented, 850 sq ft total; home office 140 sq ft exclusive

What you should report (forms and where)
- Federal Form 1040 — your main return.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report your web‑design income ($11,200) and ordinary business expenses. The home‑office deduction is reported via Schedule C.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — use this if you use the actual expense method for the home office. If you use the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829 and instead enter the simplified deduction directly on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax on net Schedule C profit.
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — student loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) and the deduction for half of your self‑employment tax are reported here (these reduce AGI).
- Schedule A — only if you itemize; charitable gifts are reported here. (More below on itemize vs standard.)
- State: Oregon return (generally OR‑40 for residents) — Oregon requires its own return and uses federal AGI as a starting point; states have different rules and deductions, so check Oregon specifics or ask your preparer.

Which items are deductible and how
- Student loan interest: deductible as an adjustment to income (above‑the‑line) up to $2,500 for 2023. You paid $2,700, so the deductible amount is limited to $2,500 (assuming your MAGI is below the phaseout range; with the income you reported you’ll be well under the 2023 phaseout for married filing jointly).
- Charitable gift ($1,350): deductible only if you itemize on Schedule A. If you take the standard deduction you do not get an extra deduction for this gift.
- “Unreimbursed expenses”:
  - If these are business expenses you incurred as a self‑employed web‑designer (supplies, software, hosting, subcontracts, etc.), report them on Schedule C and they reduce your net self‑employment income.
  - If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025.
- Interest income: report the $1,470 interest on Form 1040. (No Schedule B required unless combined interest/dividend > $1,500.)
- Home office (for your web‑design business): you can claim a deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You have two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. At 140 sq ft that’s 140 × $5 = $700. You claim that directly on Schedule C.
  - Actual expense method: allocate a percentage of your actual housing costs (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) equal to the office percentage (140/850 ≈ 16.47%). For rent alone: $2,400/mo × 12 = $28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,744 of rent attributable to the home office. You’d also allocate utilities, internet, renter’s insurance, etc. Use Form 8829 to compute and document the calculation.
  - Important limits:
    - The home‑office deduction cannot exceed your net profit from that business (it can’t create a loss from the business for purposes of this deduction; excess may carry forward in some cases).
    - For renters, you can allocate rent and utilities but you cannot depreciate the rental dwelling (depreciation is for owners).
    - If you use actual method, keep receipts/support for rent and utilities and prepare Form 8829.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C profit; you can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

Itemize vs standard deduction (practical recommendation)
- 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly: $27,700.
- Your only clearly itemizable amount provided is the $1,350 charitable gift (plus you may have state income tax or property tax withheld/paid that could increase itemized total). Unless you have large state/local taxes, mortgage interest, large medical expenses, or other sizable deductions, your itemized total will be far less than $27,700.
- Recommendation: Almost certainly take the standard deduction ($27,700) and report your charitable gift only for recordkeeping (you can still track it for future years if your situation changes). Taking the standard deduction does not prevent you from taking business deductions (Schedule C) or the student loan interest above‑the‑line deduction.

Quick illustrative tax impact (approximate; for planning only)
I’ll show three scenarios for how the home office and the $1,350 unreimbursed expense could affect taxable income. These are rough, illustrative calculations (federal only) to show the relative impact of choices; I round numbers and omit some smaller items and credits.

Base numbers:
- W‑2: $84,900
- Interest: $1,470
- Self‑employment (gross): $11,200

Scenario 1 — Simplified home office ($5/sqft = $700), no extra business expenses:
- Net self‑employment income = 11,200 − 700 = 10,500
- Total income = 84,900 + 1,470 + 10,500 = 96,870
- SE tax ≈ $1,484 (you deduct half ≈ $742 on Schedule 1)
- Student loan interest adjustment = $2,500
- AGI ≈ 96,870 − 742 − 2,500 = 93,628
- Standard deduction (MFJ) −27,700 → taxable income ≈ $65,928

Scenario 2 — Actual home office (rent allocation only) ≈ $4,744, no other business expenses:
- Net self‑employment income = 11,200 − 4,744 = 6,456
- Total income = 84,900 + 1,470 + 6,456 = 92,826
- SE tax ≈ $912 (half ≈ $456 deduction)
- AGI ≈ 92,826 − 456 − 2,500 = 89,870
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $62,170

Scenario 3 — Actual home office plus $1,350 business expenses (if those unreimbursed expenses are business expenses):
- Net self‑employment income = 11,200 − 4,744 − 1,350 = 5,106
- Total income = 84,900 + 1,470 + 5,106 = 91,476
- SE tax ≈ $721 (half ≈ $360 deduction)
- AGI ≈ 91,476 − 360 − 2,500 = 88,615
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ $60,915

Takeaways from those numbers:
- The home office actual method (rent allocation) gives a larger deduction than the simplified method in your case (because rent is high relative to the $5/sqft simplified amount).
- The home office deduction is limited by net profit from the business, so if actual home‑office allocation exceeds business income, it’s limited.
- If the $1,350 is a business expense for your web‑design work, claim it on Schedule C — that reduces both taxable income and self‑employment tax. If it’s an unreimbursed employee expense for your W‑2 job, it’s not deductible now.

Practical next steps / checklist for filing
1. Gather documents:
   - W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics.
   - Any 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC / 1099‑K for self‑employment income (even if you don’t receive a 1099 you still report the income).
   - 1099‑INTs for interest (or records showing the $1,470).
   - Form 1098‑E or other loan document showing student loan interest paid.
   - Receipts for the $1,350 charitable gift (keep the acknowledgement from the charity).
   - Rent receipts and utility bills for home office actual method (if you choose actual).
   - Records/receipts for the $1,350 unreimbursed expense and documentation whether it was for self‑employment or a W‑2 job.
2. Decide home‑office method:
   - If you want simplicity: choose the simplified method ($700).
   - If you want larger deduction and have good records of rent/utilities, compute actual expenses with Form 8829 (likely better here because of high rent).
3. Prepare Schedule C with web‑design income and business expenses (including home office).
4. Calculate Schedule SE and enter half the SE tax as adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
5. Enter student loan interest (up to $2,500) on Schedule 1.
6. Take the standard deduction on Form 1040 (likely best based on amounts you provided).
7. File federal Form 1040 and Oregon state return (OR‑40). Check Oregon’s specific rules (some states treat home office or student loan adjustments differently).
8. Keep all receipts and records for 3–7 years in case of IRS/state audit.

Other notes and cautions
- If you work as an employee and also do some self‑employment, make sure expenses are correctly allocated to Schedule C. Don’t try to deduct employee unreimbursed expenses on Schedule A — they’re suspended currently.
- Make sure you’re the borrower on the student loan interest you claim.
- If you haven’t received any 1099s but were paid by clients, still report the income.
- State rules: Oregon may have differences — check OR guidance or ask a state‑side preparer.
- This is general guidance. If you want me to prepare a worksheet, run the numbers with exact rent/utilities amounts, or draft the Schedule C Form 8829 entries, tell me which home‑office method you prefer and clarify whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are self‑employment related. If you prefer, I can also draft an email to a CPA with these details.

Would you like me to:
- run the numbers more precisely using your exact annual utility and renter’s‑insurance amounts and confirm whether actual method is better, or
- draft a list of fields to give to a preparer/CPA, or
- draft the Schedule C / Form 8829 entries based on the actual method?

