Below is a concise, practical rundown for filing your 2023 federal (and basic Oregon) tax return based on the numbers you gave. I’ll cover what you can deduct, whether to itemize or take the standard deduction, how to treat the home office (with a quick calculation), and the federal/state forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions you should check.

Quick summary of your reported 2023 income
- W‑2 (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900
- Self‑employment (web design): $11,200 → report on Schedule C
- Interest: $920 (Goldman Sachs Marcus) + $550 (TD Ameritrade) = $1,470 → report on Form 1040 (1099‑INTs)
- Total gross income ≈ $97,570

What you can (likely) deduct
- Student‑loan interest: you reported $2,700 paid in 2023. The maximum federal student‑loan interest deduction is $2,500 for 2023. You can claim up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income on your federal return (Schedule 1) provided your MAGI is below the phaseout range for married filing jointly (you’re well below typical phaseout thresholds at the income level you gave).
  - You should have Form 1098‑E from FedLoan to support this.
- Charitable gift: $1,350 to St. Mary’s Food Bank is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You have receipts, which is good.
- Unreimbursed expenses ($1,350): these are deductible only if they are business expenses for your self‑employment income. If they are business expenses (supplies, software, mileage, etc. related to the web design work), include them on Schedule C. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses related to your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible for 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA).
- Home‑office: deductible if you are self‑employed and the space is used exclusively and regularly for your business (you said it is exclusive). See details below.
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll pay self‑employment tax on your net Schedule C profit (Social Security + Medicare). You can deduct one‑half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).

Should you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- 2023 federal standard deduction for married filing jointly = $27,700.
- Your only confirmed potential itemized deduction is the $1,350 charity (plus any state/local taxes, mortgage interest, property tax, large medical expenses, etc., which you didn’t mention).
- Conclusion: With only $1,350 in charitable donations (and no mortgage interest or large other Schedule A items reported), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction ($27,700). That is almost always the right choice unless your total Schedule A deductions exceed $27,700.

Home‑office treatment (two methods) — and which is better here
Eligibility: Must be used regularly AND exclusively for your business. You said the home office is 140 sq ft exclusive; apartment is 850 sq ft → qualifies.

Two methods:
1) Simplified method
- $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
- Your deduction = 140 sq ft × $5 = $700.
- No Form 8829 required. You report $700 on Schedule C.

2) Regular (actual expenses) method
- Allocate a share of actual home expenses to the business based on the business percentage of home square footage.
- Business percentage = 140 / 850 = 0.1647 (≈16.47%).
- Annual rent = $2,400 × 12 = $28,800.
- Home office share of rent = $28,800 × 0.1647 ≈ $4,742.
- You can also allocate a share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., to increase the deduction (but you cannot depreciate rent; depreciation applies only if you own the home).
- If you use the actual method, you’ll file Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) with your Schedule C.

Which method looks better for you?
- Actual method (rent share alone ≈ $4,742) is much larger than the simplified $700, so actual method will likely give you a bigger deduction. If your rent includes utilities and you can allocate part of utilities, the deduction could be slightly higher.
- The home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit, which in turn reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax.

Important limits and notes on the home office:
- The home office deduction cannot exceed your net business income from that activity (i.e., you can’t create or increase a Schedule C loss solely from the home‑office deduction; there are carryover rules for certain situations).
- Keep documentation: floor plan/measurement, proof of exclusive use, copies of rent statements, utility bills, and records of how you allocated shared expenses.

Self‑employment tax estimate and effect
- If you take the actual home office and claim $4,742 and also claim $1,350 of business expenses (if those are business-related), your Schedule C net profit would be roughly 11,200 − (4,742 + 1,350) = $5,108.
- Self‑employment tax is computed on net earnings (approx 92.35% of net profit) × 15.3%. For a $5,108 net profit, SE tax would be roughly $720 and one‑half of that (~$360) is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Exact numbers depend on exactly which business expenses you include.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report the student loan interest deduction and the deduction for half of self‑employment tax (it flows here).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your web design income and business expenses including the home‑office deduction (or a portion depending on method).
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual expense method for the home office. NOT used if you use the simplified $5/sq ft method.
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (you’ll probably not use this unless you have additional itemizable expenses).
- Attach W‑2, and any 1099‑INT or 1099‑NEC/MISC you received.

Oregon state return
- You’ll file Oregon resident return (Form OR‑40 for most residents) for 2023. Many federal adjustments carry to Oregon but Oregon has its own rules and limitations. If you had Oregon income tax withheld (on your W‑2) it will be reported on the state return. If you owe additional federal tax because of self‑employment, you may owe more state tax too.
- If you expect to owe a significant amount of tax due to self‑employment, you may have needed to make estimated payments during 2023 — check whether you made those or whether penalties may apply.

Next steps / items to gather before filing
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics
- 1099‑INT forms for the two interest accounts
- 1098‑E for the student‑loan interest (FedLoan)
- Records/receipts for all business expenses (the $1,350 expenses), receipts for the charitable donation
- Proof of rent payments and utilities (monthly statements) and documentation demonstrating exclusive use of the 140 sq ft home office (photos/floor plan)
- Any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC you received from clients (if any)

Questions I recommend you answer (so I can give more specific guidance)
1. Are the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” business expenses for the web design (software, supplies, travel) or unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job? (If the former, they’re deductible on Schedule C; if the latter, they’re not deductible.)
2. Do you own your home or rent? (You said you pay $2,400/month — I treated that as rent, which means no depreciation; if you own, depreciation may apply.)
3. Do you have any other itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, medical expenses >7.5% of AGI, etc.)?

Final recommendations
- Most likely: use Schedule C (actual home‑office method) because the rent share gives a much larger deduction than the simplified method; include the $1,350 business expenses on Schedule C if they’re business‑related; claim up to $2,500 student loan interest as an adjustment; take the standard deduction on Form 1040 (Schedule A not needed).
- Because you have self‑employment income, use Schedule SE and make sure the deduction for half the SE tax is taken on Schedule 1.
- If you’re not comfortable preparing these forms yourself, consider using tax software (most support Schedule C, Schedule SE, and Form 8829) or consult a CPA/tax preparer—especially to ensure the home‑office allocation and business expenses are handled correctly and to confirm any Oregon specifics.

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough draft calculation of your federal tax (estimate liability/refund) using the specific assumptions above.
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect for an e‑file or preparer.
Tell me which one and confirm whether the $1,350 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses and whether you rent (not own).

