Short answer up front
- Most likely you should take the 2023 standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly ($27,700) unless you have substantial additional itemizable deductions (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, or other charitable gifts). With only the $1,350 charitable gift shown (and no mortgage interest or property tax reported), itemizing will not beat the standard deduction.
- Your web‑design income is reported on Schedule C (self‑employment). You can (and should) claim a home‑office deduction for the 140 sq ft office. Because you rent and your home‑office percentage of the house is large, the actual‑expense method (allocating rent/utilities) looks far better than the simplified method in your situation.
- You will also owe self‑employment tax on the net profit from the business (Schedule SE); you can deduct one‑half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (limit $2,500 for 2023, subject to phaseouts). If your MAGI is below the phaseout range you can deduct up to $2,500 (you reported $2,700 paid, so expect the deduction to be capped at $2,500 unless MAGI phaseouts apply).
- Charity ($1,350) is deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you cannot also deduct that charity on federal Form 1040.

Detailed explanation + sample numbers
I'll walk through the treatment of each item and show two quick scenarios (actual home‑office allocation vs simplified method) so you can see the tax effect.

1) Income
- W‑2 (Desert Sun Logistics): $84,900 (reported on Form W‑2 -> Form 1040)
- Self‑employment (web design, 2 clients): $11,200 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest from Goldman Sachs Marcus: $920 (1099‑INT → Schedule B if required)
- TD Ameritrade: $550 (likely 1099‑INT or 1099‑DIV — report on the appropriate schedule)

2) Business expenses / home office
- Home office: 140 sq ft exclusive use; total rented space = 850 sq ft → business % = 140/850 ≈ 16.47%.
- Rent: $2,400/mo → $28,800/yr. Allocated rent for office (actual method) = 28,800 × 16.47% ≈ $4,744.
- You also listed $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses.” If those are expenses for your web‑design business (software, supplies, client travel, advertising, etc.), they belong on Schedule C as ordinary business expenses. If those unreimbursed expenses are employee expenses (i.e., you paid them as an employee and were not reimbursed), they are not deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025.

Home‑office methods:
- Simplified method: $5 × 140 sq ft = $700 (no Form 8829 needed).
- Actual method: allocate your portion of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. to the business (Form 8829 is used to compute and report business‑use‑of‑home amounts). For your rent alone we estimated $4,744 (plus any allocated utilities you can document).

Which method is better here?
- Actual method (using just rent allocation + the $1,350 business expenses you listed) yields a much larger deduction than the simplified $700. So the actual method will almost certainly give you a lower taxable income. Use records for rent, utilities, and any repairs or insurance you want to allocate.

3) Self‑employment tax and above‑the‑line deduction
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on ~92.35% of net profit). You compute Schedule SE to figure the SE tax, then you deduct one‑half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040 (Schedule 1).
- You also deduct student loan interest (Form 1098‑E supports this) as an above‑the‑line adjustment (up to $2,500 for 2023, subject to MAGI phaseouts). Based on the incomes you listed, you are well below the phaseout threshold, so you’ll likely get the full $2,500 cap (you reported $2,700 paid).

4) Charitable donation
- $1,350 to St. Mary’s Food Bank — deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction you do not get a separate federal deduction for this gift. Keep the receipt.

5) Rough worked examples (rounded) — to illustrate outcome

Assumptions: the $1,350 is a business expense for the web work; interest amounts are taxable as reported.

A) Actual home‑office method (using rent allocation only + the $1,350 business expense)
- Business expenses = $4,744 (home office rent alloc.) + $1,350 = $6,094
- Net business profit = $11,200 − $6,094 = $5,106
- SE tax ≈ $5,106 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $722 → half SE deduction ≈ $361
- Total income before adjustments = W‑2 + interest + net business = 84,900 + (920+550) + 5,106 = 91,476
- AGI after above‑the‑line deductions (half SE + student loan interest $2,500 cap) ≈ 91,476 − 361 − 2,500 = 88,616
- Standard deduction (MFJ) = 27,700 → taxable income ≈ 60,916

B) Simplified home‑office method ($700) + $1,350 business expense
- Business expenses = $700 + $1,350 = $2,050
- Net business profit = $11,200 − $2,050 = $9,150
- SE tax ≈ $9,150 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $1,293 → half SE deduction ≈ $647
- Total income before adjustments = 84,900 + 1,470 + 9,150 = 95,520
- AGI after adjustments ≈ 95,520 − 647 − 2,500 = 92,373
- Taxable income after standard deduction ≈ 64,673

Conclusion from examples: Actual home‑office allocation (using rent) substantially lowers your taxable income versus the simplified method in your situation. So use actual method and complete Form 8829 (or the relevant worksheet) to report the home‑office share of rent/utilities.

6) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main tax return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments) — student loan interest & adjustments show here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report web design income and expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — calculate SE tax
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC if you received them for client payments (report the income even if you did NOT receive a 1099)
- 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV from your bank and broker for interest/dividends
- Form 1098‑E from loan servicer for student loan interest paid (to support the interest deduction)
- State: Oregon individual return (OR‑40 for full-year residents) — Oregon forms will differ; state taxable income may differ and state deductions have different rules.

7) Other points and reminders
- If the $1,350 “unreimbursed expenses” are employee expenses (not self‑employment), they are not deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025.
- Keep documentation (receipts, rent payments, utilities, charitable receipt, 1098‑E) for at least 3 years.
- If you expect similar self‑employment income in future years, consider making estimated quarterly tax payments (to cover income and self‑employment taxes) so you don’t owe penalties.
- Check your W‑2 for state tax withholdings — if you have significant Oregon withholding, that is relevant for the state return and for whether itemizing would be beneficial (SALT cap rules apply).
- If anything else exists (mortgage interest, property taxes, large unreimbursed state taxes, other donations), provide those amounts and I can re‑check whether itemizing would be better.

8) Caveat
This is general guidance based on the data you provided. Tax rules are detailed and there are additional limits and exceptions that could apply to your situation. I’m not a CPA. For filing and planning (particularly if you have complexities or larger numbers) consider reviewing these numbers with a tax professional or accountant.

